\subsection{Proof of \Cref{lem:SGT}}
\label{app:proofofSGT}
We explain how to obtain \Cref{lem:SGT} from \cite[Th.~2]{EncisoSontag_Global2006}. First,  we note that \cite{EncisoSontag_Global2006} identifies the dynamical system with its behavior, i.e., the function $x(t,x_0,u)$; while we consider the special case that the behavior is generated by the (nonsmooth) differential equation \eqref{eq:introsystem}; this results in a different notation, but no technical complication. We should only prove that the conditions in our Lemma~\ref{lem:SGT} ensure the assumptions H1, H2, H3, H4 in \cite[Th.~2]{EncisoSontag_Global2006}. H1 is satisfied because we restrict $\K_U$, $\K_X$, $\K_U$ to be orthants. H2 and H3 are guaranteed by 1), as discussed in \cite[p.~553]{EncisoSontag_Global2006}. H4 is satisfied by conditions 2) and 3), and because the continuous functions $k_x$ and $g$ must necessarily map bounded sets to bounded sets. In fact, $k_x$ and $g$  are defined over the closed sets $U$ and $X$: if $A\subseteq U$ is a bounded set, then $\operatorname{cl}(A) \subseteq U$ is compact, thus $k_x(A) \subseteq k_x(\operatorname{cl}(A))$ is bounded, since continuous functions map compact sets to compact sets; the same for $g$. 

\subsection{Proof of \Cref{prop:wellposedness}}

The closed-loop system can be written as the projected dynamical system $\dot \omega = \Pi_{\Omega} (\omega,\mathcal{A}(\omega)$), where $\omega = (x,u)$, $\Omega = \R^m \times \mathcal U$,
\begin{align}
\mathcal{A}(\omega) = \begin{bsmallmatrix}
        f(x,u)
        \\
       - \alpha \tilde H (u) \nabla \Phi (u,(g(x))
       \end{bsmallmatrix}.
\end{align}
By Assumptions~\ref{asm:plant}, \ref{asm:cost}, $\mathcal{A}$ is locally Lipschitz. Hence, for any initial condition in $\Omega$, there is a unique local solution $\omega(t) = (x(t),u(t))$, contained in $\Omega$ \cite{Cojocaru2003}. We next show  that $\omega(t)$ is contained in an invariant compact set $\mathcal P$; then, $\mathcal A$ is Lipschitz on $\mathcal P$, hence a unique global solution exists (i.e., $\omega(t)$ can be extended for all $t\geq 0)$. In particular, $u(t) \in \mathcal U$, which is compact by Assumption~\ref{asm:cost}. Furthermore, $\mathcal U$ can be rewritten as $\{u \in \R^m \mid u^{\min} \preccurlyeq_{\K_U} u \preccurlyeq_{\K_U} u^{\max}$\}, for some vector $u^{\max},u^{\min} \in\R^m$. Therefore, in view of \Cref{asm:monotone}, we can apply \cite[Prop.~V.4]{Angeli_MCS_2003}  and conclude that $x^{\min} (t)\preccurlyeq_{\K_X} x(t) \preccurlyeq_{\K_X} x^{\max}(t) $, where $x^{\min} $ and $x^{\max}$ are the solutions of \eqref{eq:plant} obtained with constant input $u^{\min}$ and $u^{\max}$, respectively. However, $x^{\min} $ and $x^{\max}$ are bounded (and global) by \Cref{asm:plant}. Hence, $x(t)$ is  contained in a bounded set $\mathcal P_x$. The conclusion follows by choosing $\mathcal P$ as a compact set containing $\mathcal P_x \times \mathcal P$.

\subsection{Proof of \Cref{th:main}}
We start by showing that the  system \eqref{eq:clreformulatedx}-\eqref{eq:clreformulatedv} is monotone with respect to $K_U$, $\K_\Omega \coloneqq\K_X\times \K_V$, $K_Z$, and that it admits a well-defined steady-state map. 
These are known property for the cascade of monotone systems, e.g.,  \cite[Prop.~IV.I]{Angeli_MCS_2003}; we include a proof for completeness. 

First, choose arbitrary $u(t) \seq_{\K_U} u'(t)$ for almost all $t$ and $\omega_0 = (x_0,v_0) \seq_{\K_\Omega} \omega_0' =  (x_0',v_0')$, i.e., $x_0 \seq_{\K_X} x_0'$ and $v_0 \seq_{\K_V} v_0'$. Then, by \Cref{asm:plant}, $x(t)= x(t,x_0,u) \seq_{\K_X} x'(t) = x(t,x_0',u)$; hence, also $v(t,v_0,x) \seq_{\K_V} v(t,v_0',x')$ by \Cref{asm:core}(ii). In other words, $\omega(t,\omega_0,u) \seq_{\K_\Omega} \omega(t,\omega_0',u') $. Also, for the output map $z = \phi(\omega) \coloneqq v$, it  holds that  $\phi (\omega) \seq_{\K_Z}  \phi(\omega')$. In conclusion,  \eqref{eq:clreformulatedx}-\eqref{eq:clreformulatedv} is monotone with respect to $K_U$, $\K_\Omega$, $K_Z$.

Second, for a constant input $u \equiv \bar u$, \eqref{eq:clreformulatedx} converges to $k_x(\bar u)$ by \Cref{asm:plant}, hence $v$ converges to $k_v(k_x)$ by \cite[Th.~1]{EncisoSontag_Global2006} and \Cref{asm:core}. Since $k_v$ and $k_x$ are continuous, the steady-state map for the system \eqref{eq:clreformulatedx}-\eqref{eq:clreformulatedv} $k_\omega(u) = (k_x(u),k_v(k_x(u)))$ is also continuous. 

We can now apply \Cref{lem:SGT} (in particular, note that $\K_Z = - \K_U$ by \Cref{asm:core}, and $\Omega$ is a box set): we can conclude global convergence of \eqref{eq:clreformulated} if the iteration $u_{n+1}=k_z(u_n)$ has a global attractor. To retrieve \Cref{asm:core}(iii) from this condition, we compute the input-output steady-state map 
$k_z$ of \eqref{eq:clreformulatedx}-\eqref{eq:clreformulatedv}. To start, the steady-state map $\bar v = k_v(x)$ of \eqref{eq:clreformulatedv} computed at any fixed $x$ has to satisfy   
\begin{align}
    \0 & = \Pi_{\mathcal U} \left(\bar v, - \alpha H(\bar v) \nabla \Phi (\bar v,g(x)) \right)
    \\ & =  \Pi_{\mathcal U} \left(\bar v, - \alpha \nabla \phi_u(\bar v) - \alpha \nabla h(\bar v)^\top  \nabla \phi_y(g(x)) \right). \label{eq:usefulGradient}
\end{align}
Note that \eqref{eq:usefulGradient} corresponds to the equilibrium condition for the projected gradient flow applied to the function $\psi(\cdot) \coloneqq \phi_u(\cdot)+ h(\cdot)^\top \nabla \phi_y(g(x))$; hence, $\bar v = k_v(x)$ is the unique critical point of $\psi$ over $\mathcal U$ (otherwise the steady-state map would not be defined, violating  \Cref{asm:core}(i)). Since $\mathcal U$ is compact, $\psi$ achieves a minimum in $\mathcal U$, which must be a critical point. Hence, 
\begin{align}
    k_v(x) = \argmin_{v\in\mathcal U} \phi_u(v)+ h(v)^\top \nabla \phi_y(g(x)).
\end{align}
Therefore, by recalling that $g\circ k_x = k_y$ and since $z =v$, we can compute $k_z(u) = k_v(k_x(u))$ as 
\begin{align}
    k_z(u) = \argmin_{v\in \mathcal U} \phi_u(v)+ h(v)^\top \nabla \phi_y(k_y(u)).
\end{align}


\subsection{Proofs of Section~\ref{sec:special}}



We will show the condition in \Cref{asm:core}(i) with respect to  $\K_X = \R^n_{\geq 0} $, $\K_V =\R^{m}_{\leq_0}$, $\K_Z = \R^{m}_{\leq_0}$.  We use the following result. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:infinitesimal}
    Let $q(v,x) \coloneqq -H(v)\nabla \Phi(v,g(x)) = -\nabla \Phi_u(v)  -\nabla k_y(v)^\top \nabla \Phi_y(g(x))$. If 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:conditionInfinitesimal}
        v \preccurlyeq v', \ x\seq x' \Rightarrow q(v,x) - q(v',x') \ \in \mathrm{T}_{\R^m_{\leq 0}} (v-v'),
    \end{align}
    then the system in \eqref{eq:clreformulatedv} is monotone with respect to the orthants $\K_X = \R^n_{\geq 0} $, $\K_V =\R^{m}_{\leq_0}$, $\K_Z = \R^{m}_{\leq_0}$.
\end{lemma} 

\begin{proof}
    The result is based on the analogous for smooth ODEs \cite[Th.~1]{Angeli_MCS_2003}, where the extension to the projected dynamics \eqref{eq:clreformulatedv} only requires checking the discontinuity points of $\dot v$, by exploiting the fact that $\mathcal{U}$ is a box. We only sketch the proof. 

    First, since $\mathcal U$ is a box, the projection on its tangent cone \eqref{eq:explicit_ptc} is order preserving. In particular, by \eqref{eq:conditionInfinitesimal}, it holds pointwise that
    \begin{align*}
    \begin{multlined}
        v \preccurlyeq v', x \succcurlyeq x' \\ \Rightarrow \Pi_{\mathcal{U}}(v,q(v,x)) - \Pi_{\mathcal{U}}(v',q(v',x'))\in \mathrm{T}_{\R^m_{\leq 0}}(v-v'). 
        \end{multlined}
    \end{align*}
    Consider the solutions $v(t,v_0,x)$ and $v'(t,v_0',x')$, with $x(t) 
    \seq x'(t)$ for almost all $t$, $v_0 \preccurlyeq v_0'$. For contradiction, assume there is $t$ such that $v(t) \not\preccurlyeq v'(t)$, i.e., there is $v_i(t) > v_i'(t)$, for some $i \in \{1,\dots,m\}$. Choose $ t_0 = 
    \inf \{ t \mid v(t) \not\preccurlyeq v'(t)\}$, so that $v_i(t_0) >v_i'(t_0)$ in ($t_0,t_0+\epsilon)$ for some $\epsilon>0$, and $v_i(t_0) = v_i(t_0) = u_i^{\max}$ (or another point on the boundary of $\mathcal U$ where discontinuity can happen). This means $\dot v_i'<0$ almost everywhere for $(t_0,t_0+\epsilon)$, meaning  that $t_0$ cannot be a discontinuity point (as this would require either $\dot v_i'(t)>0$ or $\dot v_i(t) >0$ in $t\in (t_0-\epsilon, t_0)$, but this implies the former since $v_i(t) \leq v_i'(t)$ for $t \leq t_0$).  
\end{proof}
Note that the condition in \Cref{lem:infinitesimal} needs to be checked only if $[v]_i = [v']_i$, for some $i \in \{ 1, \dots, m\}$.

\subsection{Proof of \Cref{lem:asmi}}
    \emph{(i)} Since $m = 1$, we just need to check \eqref{eq:conditionInfinitesimal} for $v=v'$. However, $k_y$ is nondecreasing, since it is the steady-state map of a monotone system \cite[Rem~V.2]{Angeli_MCS_2003}, hence $\nabla k_y(v)$ is nonnegative for all $v$; $\nabla \Phi_y$ is nondecreasing by convexity; $g(x)$ is also nondecreasing by \Cref{asm:monotone}. Therefore $x \seq x' \Rightarrow q(v,x) - q(v,x') 
    \leq 0$, and the conclusion follows by \Cref{lem:infinitesimal}. 
    \emph{(ii)} The matrix $S$ must be nonnegative by \cite[Rem~V.2]{Angeli_MCS_2003} and \Cref{asm:monotone}; $g$ is also nondecreasing by \Cref{asm:monotone}. The condition imposed are sufficient to prove that $\nabla \Phi_u$ and $\nabla \Phi_y$ are also nondecreasing \cite{Angeli2004} (in the standard order).  Therefore, we can prove that condition \eqref{eq:conditionInfinitesimal} holds separately for the two components in $q(v,x)$, namely $-\Phi_u(v)$ and $- S^\top \nabla \Phi_y(g(x))$. 

    \subsection{Proof of \Cref{lem:asmii}} 
    \emph{(i)} If the function $\Psi$ is strictly convex, then its projected gradient flow \eqref{eq:clreformulatedv} converges to the unique minimizer, for any fixed $x\in \R^n$. Furthermore, $x\mapsto \argmin_{u\in\mathcal U} \Psi(u,x)$ is a continuous function whenever $\mathcal U$ is compact and $\Psi$ is continuous and has a unique minimizer for each $x$, see \cite[Lem.~3]{Bianchi_Mon_CDC_2022} for an analogous proof (where strong convexity  replaces the role of compactness). \emph{(ii)} This is a sufficient condition for (i). 

   \subsection{Proof of \Cref{lem:asmiii}}
1) For any $w,w' \in \mathcal U$, let $u = \argmin_{v\in \mc{U}} \tilde\Psi (v,w)$ and $u' = \argmin_{v\in \mc{U}} \tilde\Psi (v,w')$. We have the first order optimality relations 
\begin{align*}
    \langle \nabla_u \tilde\Psi (u,w), u-u' \rangle & \geq 0
    \\ 
     \langle \nabla_u \tilde\Psi (u',w'), u-u' \rangle & \geq 0.
\end{align*}
Summing app and adding and subtracting $ \nabla_u \tilde\Psi (u,w')$ on the left side, we obtain
$\langle \nabla_u \tilde\Psi (u,w) -\nabla_u \tilde\Psi (u,w'), u'-u\rangle  + \langle \nabla_u \tilde\Psi (u,w')  - \nabla_u \tilde\Psi (u',w), u'-u \rangle  \geq 0$. By using the Cauchy--Schwartz inequality and smoothness for the first addend, and strong convexity for the second, we obtain
\begin{align*}
      & \ell \| w -w'\|\|u-u'\| - \mu \| u - u'\| ^2 \geq 0
      \\ \Rightarrow  &  \| u-u'\| \leq \textstyle  \frac{\ell}{\mu} \|w -w'\|. \numberthis \label{eq:contractivity}
\end{align*}

