%\textcolor{blue}{ While UAV trajectory optimization offers high flexibility and Line-of-Sight (LoS) advantages, it suffers from significant energy consumption, latency, and complex trajectory optimization. Conversely, movable antenna (MA) technology provides fine-grained spatial reconfiguration (antenna positioning within a confined area) with ultra-low energy overhead and millisecond-scale response, enabling real-time channel manipulation and covert beam steering. To investigates the potential of MA-enabled micro-mobility to replace UAV-enabled macro-mobility for enhancing physical layer security (PLS) in air-to-ground communications, we conduct a systematic comparative study between the two paradigms in terms of secrecy performance. Specially, we establish a dual-scale mobility framework where a UAV-mounted uniform linear array (ULA) serves as a base station transmitting confidential information to a legitimate user (Bob) in the presence of an eavesdropper (Eve).}
This paper investigates the potential of movable antenna (MA)-enabled micro-mobility to replace UAV-enabled macro-mobility for enhancing physical layer security (PLS) in air-to-ground communications. While UAV trajectory optimization offers high flexibility and Line-of-Sight (LoS) advantages, it suffers from significant energy consumption, latency, and complex trajectory optimization. Conversely, MA technology provides fine-grained spatial reconfiguration (antenna positioning within a confined area) with ultra-low energy overhead and millisecond-scale response, enabling real-time channel manipulation and covert beam steering.
To systematically compare these paradigms, we establish a dual-scale mobility framework where a UAV-mounted uniform linear array (ULA) serves as a base station transmitting confidential information to a legitimate user (Bob) in the presence of an eavesdropper (Eve). We formulate non-convex average secrecy rate (ASR) maximization problems for both schemes: 1) MA-based micro-mobility: Jointly optimizing antenna positions and beamforming (BF) vectors under positioning constraints; 2) UAV-based macro-mobility: Jointly optimizing the UAV's trajectory and BF vectors under kinematic constraints. 
Extensive simulations reveal distinct operational regimes: MA micro-mobility demonstrates significant ASR advantages in low-transmit-power scenarios or under antenna constraints due to its energy-efficient spatial control. Conversely, UAV macro-mobility excels under resource-sufficient conditions (higher power, larger antenna arrays) by leveraging global mobility for optimal positioning. The findings highlight the complementary strengths of both approaches, suggesting hybrid micro-macro mobility as a promising direction for balancing security, energy efficiency, and deployment complexity in future wireless networks.
%Unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) trajectory planning has garnered significant attention in air-to-ground secure communication, driven by its high flexibility and line-of-sight communication. However, the macroscopic movement of UAVs entails substantial energy expenditure and considerable trajectory optimization complexity. Meanwhile, movable antenna (MA) arrays provide a compelling alternative through microscopic spatial reconfiguration, leveraging fine-grained positional shifts to improve secrecy without requiring full-platform mobility. To fundamentally investigate the feasibility of transitioning from UAV-enabled macroscopic mobility to MA-driven microscopic motion paradigms, we establish an air-to-ground communication model aiming to explore the performance gaps between micro- and macro-mobility schemes. First, a maximum average secrecy rate framework is formulated by jointly designing the MA's position/UAV's trajectory and the beamforming (BF), and the complex non-convex problems are solved via the proposed alternating optimization algorithms, respectively. Subsequently, a performance gap function is proposed to characterize the differences between the two approaches. Simulation results reveal that the MA-based design demonstrates significant advantages in low-power or antenna-constrained scenarios, while the UAV-based scheme excels under resource-sufficient conditions. The complementary strengths of both schemes offer a promising hybrid direction for balancing security, energy efficiency, and deployment complexity in future mobile wireless networks.
\end{abstract}
