We give here an informal account of CFMs, as they are not needed for
our technical developments. A formal definition can be found in \cite{BolligFG21}.
Recall that we fixed the set $\Procs$ of processes.
In a CFM, every process $p \in\Procs$ is represented by a finite-state machine $\CFM_p$,
which allows it to execute actions from $\Act_p$ and to send and receive messages from
a set $\Msg$. Messages in transit from process $p$ to process $q$ are stored in the
(unbounded) FIFO channel $(p,q)$. However, like for HMSCs, they do not occur in (complete) MSCs.
 In addition, we have simple global acceptance conditions, which take into account
finite and/or infinite behaviors. The language of a CFM $\CFM$ is denoted by $L(\CFM)$.

