this is an instance of the bottleneck shortest path problem.
When the lattice of subgroups is given, or otherwise sufficiently understood, this can be solved using Djikstra's algorithm.
However, the number of subgroups of a finite group may be exponential in its order, and computing the lattice is generally a costly operation.

We now establish some basic properties of the Galois width, culminating in~\Cref{thm:compute-bot} which shows that it can be expressed straightforwardly in terms of group-theoretic primitives.
Clearly, for any finite group $G$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:trivial-bound}
1 \le \botn (G) \le |G|.
\end{equation}

These trivial lower and upper bounds are tight precisely when $G= \operatorname{id}$ and when $G$ is cyclic of prime order, respectively.

\begin{prop}\label{prop:bottleneck-normal-recursion}
For any normal subgroup $N\unlhd G$, we have that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bottleneck-normal-subgroup-formula}
\botn (G) = \max \left( \botn (G/N), \botn (N) \right).
\end{equation}
