For $m_i \in \R^n, C_i \in \Sym^{++}(n)$, $i=0,1$, consider the Gaussian measures $\mu_i=\Ncal(m_i,C_i)$ with densities with respect to the Lebesgue measure
%\begin{align*}
$p_i(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{(2\pi)^n\det(C_i)}}\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2}(x-m_i)^TC_i^{-1}(x-m_i)\right)$.
%\end{align*}
%$i=0,1$.
Their $\alpha$-weighted geometric mixture is the Gaussian measure with density \cite[Corollary 1]{nielsen2019geometricJensenShannon}
