	By assumption, $C_1 = C_{*}^{1/2}(I-S_1)C_{*}^{1/2}$. Since $\ker(C_{*}) = 0$, the operator 
	$(I-S_1)^{1/2}C_{*}^{1/2}$ admits the polar decomposition
	\begin{align*}
		(I-S_1)^{1/2}C_{*}^{1/2} = V(C_{*}^{1/2}(I-S_1)C_{*}^{1/2})^{1/2} = VC_{1}^{1/2},
	\end{align*}
	where $V \in \Ubb(\Hcal)$. Since $C_1 \in \Sym(\Hcal)$, it follows that
	\begin{align*}
		C_{1}^{1/2} = V^{*}(I-S_1)^{1/2}C_{*}^{1/2} = C_{*}^{1/2}(I-S_1)^{1/2}V.
	\end{align*}
	With $C_2 = C_{*}^{1/2}(I-S_2)C_{*}^{1/2}$, it follows that
	\begin{align*}
