\section{Introduction}

% LLMs-assisted automated algorithm design 
Large Language Models (LLMs) assisted iterative search methods have been used for automated algorithm design with great success~\cite{liu2023algorithm}~\cite{liu2024evolution}~\cite{romera2024mathematical}~\cite{ye2024reevo}~\cite{van2024llamea}~\cite{bomer2025leveraging}. These methods cast an algorithm design task as an optimization problem for finding an optimal algorithm in an algorithm space. Leveraging the LLM capabilities in language comprehension, reasoning and code generation, they are able to conduct effective and efficient search in an algorithm space~\cite{liu2024systematic}. However, there is lack of good understanding of why and when these methods work or not. Traditional math analysis can be hardly used to study these methods due to the complexity of the algorithm space~\cite{zhang2024understanding} and the black-box nature of LLM~\cite{allen2023physics}. Fitness landscape analysis~\cite{malan2013survey,zou2022survey,ochoa2014local,huang2025fitness,rodrigues2022fitness,de2024landscape} is a widely used methodology for investigating behaviors of search methods, particularly, in the area of heuristics. Very recently, \cite{van2025code} has made a very first attempt to study the landscape of LLM-assisted algorithm design methods. They introduced abstract syntax tree (AST)-based code evolution graphs (CEGs) and investigated the relationship between candidate algorithms (i.e., an element in the algorithm space) and their characters in feature space. However, their work does not discuss the fitness of a candidate algorithm~\footnote{For the sake of clarity, we call an algorithm in the algorithm space a candidate algorithm}. 



In this paper, we focus on the fitness landscape of LLM-assisted Algorithm Search (LAS). Leveraging a graph-based approach, we try to visualize and analyze the fitness landscape, providing deeper insights into the search dynamics of LAS. Specifically, we try to answer the following questions:



\begin{itemize}
\vspace{5pt}
\item \textbf{Q1:} What does the fitness landscape of LLM-driven algorithm search look like, and what features (e.g., plateaus, ruggedness) characterize it?
\vspace{2pt}
\item \textbf{Q2:} How does the landscape vary across different algorithm design tasks and LLMs?
\vspace{2pt}
\item \textbf{Q3:} How do the search framework and its settings influence the landscape, and how is this connected to performance?
\vspace{5pt}
\end{itemize}



Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We frame LLM-assisted Algorithm Search (LAS) as an optimization problem over algorithm space, where each algorithm is often represented as code (code implementation) and/or thought (algorithm idea description).
    \item We present a graph-based approach to visualize and analyze the fitness landscape to enhance our understanding of this significant but rarely studied topic. Each node represents one algorithm in the search process, and the edges are the connections between algorithms during the search. 
    \item We conduct a comprehensive evaluation across 6 distinct algorithm design tasks and 6 different LLMs. We find the fitness landscape of LLM-assisted algorithm search is rugged and usually multi-modal with distinct patterns and characteristics across different algorithm design tasks and LLMs, providing insights for designing more effective LAS methods.
\end{itemize}


\section{Formulation of LLM-assisted Algorithm Search}

We formulate the LLM-assisted automatic Algorithm Search (LAS) as follows. Consider a target algorithm design task $T$ (e.g., design a heuristic for the Traveling Salesman Problem) with a set of instances $I$, where $I$ represents a practical distribution of real-world scenarios. The objective is to generate an optimal algorithm $a^*$ within an algorithm space $A^{S}$ that minimizes (without loss of generality) a performance metric $F(a, I)$, defined as the sum of candidate performance scores $f(a, i)$ across all instances $i \in I$. Mathematically, this is expressed as:

$$
F(a, I) = \sum_{i \in I} f(a, i), \quad a \in A^{S}.
$$

The optimal algorithm $a^*$ is obtained by solving the following optimization problem:

$$
a^* = \underset{a \in A^{S}}{\arg\min} \, F(a, I).
$$

The optimization problem in algorithm space is usually tackled by an iterative search pipeline~\cite{liu2024systematic,zhang2024understanding}, where each algorithm is represented as code and/or text. Starting from an initial population of algorithms $P_0$, the process iteratively conducts three key steps—generation, evaluation, and update—until a stopping criterion is met. A general description of the pipeline is as follows: 

\textbf{Step 1: Initialization} The initial population of algorithms $P_0$ is generated either by prompting LLMs or by using existing hand-crafted algorithms. While some implementations (e.g., gradient-based search and neighborhood methods) use a population size of one, most LAS works employing evolutionary frameworks typically maintain larger population sizes. %This population represents all the current recorded algorithms during the search process.

\textbf{Step 2: Iterative Process} For the current algorithm population $P_i$, the iterative process consists of three sub-steps:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Step 2.1: Generation} Pre-trained LLMs are prompted based on instructions and existing parent candidates $a_p$, which are selected from the current population $P_i$. This can be represented as $a_o = LLM(Prompt(a_p))$, where $a_p$ and  $a_o$ denote the parent and offspring candidates, respectively, and $LLM$ represents the LLM inference process guided by instructions. $Prompt()$ is the instruction used for LLMs.

    \item \textbf{Step 2.2: Evaluation} The fitness $F(a, I)$ of each offspring $a$ candidate is computed over a set of representative instances $I$. The average performance is usually used as fitness.

    \item \textbf{Step 2.3: Update} The population is updated to $P_{t+1}$ by incorporating new candidates $a_o$ into $P_t$, based on some population management strategies (e.g., elitism, generational replacement).
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 3: Termination} The search process terminates when a stopping criterion is met. This criterion could be a maximum number of iterations, a maximum number of LLM requests, a performance threshold, or other problem-specific conditions. Upon termination, the best-performing algorithm $\hat{a}$, along with any other relevant information, is recorded.




\begin{algorithm}[]
\SetAlgoLined
\KwIn{Initial population $P_0$, Instructions for LLMs, Stopping criterion}
\KwOut{Best-performing algorithm $a^*$ and relevant information}

\BlankLine
\textbf{Step 1: Initialization} \\
$P \gets P_0$ \tcp*{Initialize population with $P_0$ (from LLMs or existing algorithms)}

\BlankLine
\textbf{Step 2: Iterative Process} \\
\While{not Stopping criterion}{
        $a_p \gets P$ \tcp*{Select parent from $P$}
        $a_o \gets LLM(Prompt(a_p))$ \tcp*{Generate offspring using LLMs and parent $a_p$}
        $F(a_o, I) \gets Evaluate(a_o, I)$ \tcp*{Evaluate on instances $I$}
        $P \gets UpdatePopulation(P, a_o)$ \tcp*{Update population with new offspring}

}

\BlankLine
\textbf{Step 3: Termination} \\
%\Return{Best algorithm $a^*$} % \tcp*{Return the best-performing algorithm and relevant info}
\caption{LAS Pipeline}
\end{algorithm}


