\subsection{Algorithm Representation}

Prior to the advent of LLMs, most existing works~\cite{stutzle2018automated,pillay2021automated,ma2024toward} on automated algorithm design can be classified into two categories based on their approaches for defining the algorithm search space $a \in A^S$: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] \textbf{Regular continuous/discrete space:} $A^S$ is parameterized as a (continuous, discrete, or mixed) parameter space~\cite{kerschke2019automated}, which can be solved as a classic optimization problem.
    \item[(ii)] \textbf{Tree-based/structure-based space:} Each algorithm $a$ is expressed as a tree or graph structure, and search methods such as genetic programming can be used to find a better algorithm~\cite{langdon2013foundations,mei2022explainable,zhang2023survey}.
\end{itemize}
These representations have been extensively studied. Many well-established optimization techniques can be used.

Unlike traditional approaches, LLM-assisted algorithm search represents each algorithm $a$ using a code implementation~\cite{hemberg2024evolving} and/or linguistic description~\cite{liu2024evolution}. This representation provides a richer, more expressive search space than conventional methods. However, because this space is not rigidly structured, it is challenging to understand the landscape and navigate it effectively. Figure~\ref{fig:representation} contrasts three distinct algorithm representations: (1) regular continuous/discrete space, (2) tree-based/structure-based space, and (3) code-based/text-based space.



\subsection{Algorithm Generation}
In the context of LLM-assisted algorithm search, the generation of an algorithm can be expressed as:

$$
a_o = \text{LLM}(\text{Prompt}(a_p)).
$$
Here, the offspring algorithm $a_o$ is produced by prompting the LLM with specific instructions $\text{Prompt}()$, using parent algorithms $a_p$ as input. In a single-point iterative search, one offspring is generated from a single parent in each iteration. In contrast, population-based search maintains and evolves a population of algorithms, where both the number of parent and offspring algorithms typically exceeds one. Both the prompt strategies employed and the capabilities of the LLMs will influence the algorithm generation and thus may lead to different landscapes and performance.



\subsection{Evaluation}

The evaluation $F(a, I)$ maps each algorithm $a$ to an indicator of its performance (e.g., the average route length on TSP instances). The evaluation is conducted on a set of representative instances $I$. Without loss of generality, in this paper, we consider the minimization problem.

\section{Graph-Based Fitness Landscape}


\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/Fig_illustration.pdf}
    \caption{An illustration of a two-dimensional graph representation of fitness landscape, where nodes and edges are algorithms and their connections, respectively. The color of each node indicates the objective value (the smaller the better). Each algorithm is a constructive heuristic for the traveling salesman problem. The constructive heuristic determines how to select the next node to iteratively construct a solution. The inputs "node\_c", "node\_d", "nodes\_u" and "dis" are the current node ID, destination node ID, unvisited node IDs and distance matrix, respectively. The output is the next node ID.}
    \label{fig:illustration}
\end{figure*}

This section introduces a graph-based approach to studying the fitness landscape of LLM-assisted algorithm search. We begin by defining the concept of fitness landscapes and then elaborate on their graph-based representation.

A fitness landscape, as defined by~\cite{stadler2002fitness}, can be represented using a triplet $(S, NS, f)$, where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $S$ is the set of potential solutions, representing the search space;
    \item $NS$ is the neighborhood structure, a function that assigns to each solution $s \in S$ a set of neighbors $N(s)$;
    \item $f: S \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ is the objective function (fitness function), which quantifies the quality of each solution.
\end{itemize}

In the context of LLM-assisted algorithm search, the search space $S$ comprises candidate algorithms generated by LLMs. The objective function $f$ is defined by specific performance metrics relevant to the task at hand. Since algorithms lack a well-defined distance metric or neighbourhood structure~\cite{ochoa2014local}, the neighbourhood is instead characterized by correlations between algorithms. Following the approach in~\cite{kinnear1994fitness}, we define the neighborhood of an algorithm $a_i$ relative to another algorithm $a_j$ as follows: $a_i \in NS(a_j)$ if $a_i$ is generated from $a_j$ (i.e., $a_i = LLM(Prompt(a_j))$). This formulation enables the introduction of a graph-based representation for illustrating the fitness landscape.

The graph-based representation is defined as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Nodes: } Each node in the graph represents an algorithm $a_i$ generated during the search process. The set of all nodes in the graph is denoted by $N$, with its size represented as $n$.
    \item \textbf{Edges: } Edges in the graph are directed and represent the genetic relationship between a parent algorithm and its offspring. Specifically, an edge exists from algorithm $a_i$ to algorithm $a_j$ if $a_j$ is directly derived from $a_i$ through the application of LLM-based search operators, such as $a_j = LLM(Prompt(a_i))$. Here, $Prompt()$ can involve LLM-based crossover~\cite{liu2023algorithm}, mutation~\cite{liu2023algorithm}, or other prompt strategies~\cite{liu2024evolution,ye2024reevo}. Edges are weighted based on the frequency or likelihood of the transition, where the weight corresponds to the number of times the transition (i.e., the generation of $a_j$ from $a_i$) occurs during the search process.
    \item \textbf{Graph: } The fitness landscape is represented as a graph $G = (N, E)$, where $N$ is the set of nodes corresponding to algorithms, and $E$ is the set of directed edges denoting parent-offspring relationships. Edge weights reflect transition probabilities between nodes. We consider an undirected graph, where there is no direction between $a_i$ and $a_j$ if they are connected.
\end{itemize}

Figure~\ref{fig:illustration} illustrates a two-dimensional graph representing a fitness landscape, where nodes (colored by fitness value) denote algorithms and edges represent their connections. Each algorithm is a constructive heuristic for the traveling salesman problem, taking inputs like node IDs and a distance matrix to determine the next node in the solution.


\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Date Collection}
We demonstrate it on EoH~\cite{liu2024evolution,liu2023algorithm}, a commonly used LLM-assisted algorithm search framework based on evolutionary search. We run all the experiments on LLM4AD~\footnote{https://github.com/Optima-CityU/llm4ad} Platform~\cite{liu2024llm4ad} across different tasks and LLMs. The experimental settings are as follows:

\paragraph{Evolutionary Search Settings}
The population size of EoH is set to be 20 and the maximum number of candidate evaluations is 2,000 for each run.

\paragraph{Tasks}
We investigate six algorithm design tasks including four heuristic algorithm design tasks for combinatorial optimization problems (Online Bin Packing (\textbf{OBP})~\cite{romera2024mathematical}, Traveling Salesman Problem (\textbf{TSP})~\cite{liu2023algorithm}, Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem (\textbf{CVRP})~\cite{liu2024llm4ad}, and Vehicle Routing Problem with Time Windows (\textbf{VRPTW}))~\cite{liu2024llm4ad}, one agent design task for the car mountain system (\textbf{Car})~\cite{liu2024llm4ad,brockman2016openai} and one symbolic regression task aimed at approximating the curve of bacterial growth (\textbf{Bact})~\cite{shojaee2025llmsr}. Our goal is to explore tasks across diverse domains, and we consider the symbolic regression task as a form of simple algorithm design.

\paragraph{LLMs}
We tested a diverse set of commonly used LLMs of different sizes and capabilities, including GPT-3.5-Turbo, GPT-4o-Mini, DeepSeek-V3, DeepSeek-Chat, Qwen2-Turbo, and GLM-3-Turbo.



We collect all the algorithms and record the parent-offspring algorithm connections during evolutionary search. We will have one graph for each run for fitness landscape analysis.

