\subsection{Metrics}



The following metrics were employed to evaluate the features and characteristics of the graph:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Density}: This metric quantifies the density of connections in a graph, defined as the ratio of the number of edges to the maximum possible number of edges. It ranges from 0 (no edges) to 1 (fully connected graph). Density provides insights into the sparsity or compactness of the network, with higher values indicating greater connectivity.

\item \textbf{Average Degree}: The average degree represents the mean number of edges per node in the graph. It is computed by summing the degrees of all nodes and dividing by the total number of nodes. This metric reflects the overall connectivity of the graph, where a higher average degree signifies a more interconnected network.

\item \textbf{Clustering Coefficient}: This metric captures the tendency of nodes to form tightly-knit clusters. It is defined as the ratio of the number of triangles (closed triplets) to the number of possible triplets in the graph. The clustering coefficient ranges from 0 (no clustering) to 1 (fully clustered), indicating the presence of local community structures within the network.


\end{itemize}


\subsection{Landscape on Different Tasks}
In this section, we illustrate and analyze the fitness landscapes across different algorithm design tasks. Figure~\ref{fig:tasks} illustrates the 2D and 3D visualizations of the fitness landscapes for five distinct tasks. In the 3D visualizations, the z-axis represents fitness values, where smaller (darker) values indicate better performance. The red node denotes the algorithm with the lowest fitness value. Figure~\ref{fig:distribution} shows the probability distribution of algorithm fitness values, which are normalized to the range [0, 1].

