\hypertarget{train-on-documents}{%
\subsubsection{II. TRAIN ON DOCUMENTS}\label{train-on-documents}}

If we have a structured and comprehensive description of the database, including table names, descriptions, relationships, and metadata, we can leverage this data to enhance Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) using a \textbf{Vector Database (Vector DB).}

A \textbf{Vector DB} stores documents in a high-dimensional vector space, transforming each document into numerical representations (embeddings) that capture semantic meanings. These embeddings allow for more intelligent and context-aware searches, significantly improving how information is retrieved. Each document, representing information about a table, is stored as a \textbf{vector embedding}. The embedding is generated based on multiple document features.

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=3.56812in,height=3.62609in]{images/image_1.png}

\emph{Vector DB storage approach for database documentation.}
\end{center}

These embeddings are accompanied by \textbf{metadata} that includes complete table information, relationships, and the source of embeddings. Metadata ensures the search space can be precisely controlled, enabling efficient and accurate retrieval of relevant database details.

The \textbf{training process} involves structuring documentation data into multiple embeddings to capture different aspects of the database:

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \textbf{Table Extraction} – The function leverages a \textbf{Large Language Model (LLM)} to identify and extract table names mentioned in the document.
\item
  \textbf{Description Generation} – It dynamically creates short and long structured descriptions using predefined templates.
\item
  \textbf{Column Processing} – It processes table column details and attaches them to the training data.
\item
  \textbf{Multiple Training Entries} – Each document is transformed into different categories of embeddings:
  \begin{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Full documentation} with table descriptions.
  \item
    \textbf{Dependency and relationship-based embeddings} (for tracking how tables are linked).
  \item
    \textbf{Table metadata embeddings} (name, descriptions, and keys).
  \item
    \textbf{Connected tables and their associations.}
  \item
    \textbf{Entity relationships} to understand interactions between database objects.
  \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\hypertarget{train-based-on-examples}{%
\subsection{Train based on Examples}\label{train-based-on-examples}}

While the documentation offers a comprehensive overview of the database, including relationships, connections, and other intricacies, it does not ensure that this information alone is sufficient to accurately interpret domain-specific nuances and generate the correct SQL query in response to a user's request. That is why the ability to work with \textbf{question-answering examples} is crucial.

Each example consists of a \textbf{"Question - SQL Query"} pair, which we store in the \textbf{Vector Database (Vector DB).} These examples serve as reference points, allowing the system to retrieve relevant patterns and structures when generating SQL queries in response to user requests. By leveraging these stored examples, the model can improve accuracy and adapt to complex, domain-specific queries more effectively.

To enhance \textbf{SQL query generation} and \textbf{natural language understanding}, we implemented a structured \textbf{normalization and extraction process}. This process performs the conversion of the user request, which is accompanied by an answer in view of SQL code, into a structured JSON format that captures key elements like \textbf{normalized questions, requested entities, data sources, and calculations}.

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=6.58529in,height=2.51563in]{images/image_2.png}

\emph{Structure of examples database with vector representations.}
\end{center}

Each stored example $\mathcal A_k$ is represented as structure:

$$
\mathcal A_k = (\mathcal{I}_k, \mathcal S_{code_k}, \mathcal N_k, \mathcal M_k, \mathcal E_k, \mathcal V_k, \mathcal O_k);\space \space \mathcal A_k \in \bold A;\space \space  \mathcal V_k = \{T_{ik},\bold C_{ik}\}
$$

where:

$\mathcal I_k$ — The \textbf{initial user query} for $k$ example.

$\mathcal S_{code_k}$ — The corresponding \textbf{SQL query}, if available.

$\mathcal N_k$ — The \textbf{normalized form} of the initial question.

$\mathcal E_k$ — The \textbf{extracted entities} relevant to the query.

$\mathcal M_k$ — The \textbf{main clause} of the query.

$\mathcal V_k$ — The \textbf{identified data sources} (tables and columns).

$\mathcal O_k$ — The \textbf{operations} (aggregations, filters, grouping) present in the query.

${T_{ik}} \in \bold T$  — The $i$-th \textbf{table} from the database that are used in $k$ example

$\bold C_{ik}-$  set of columns from $T_i$ that are used in $k$ example

\hypertarget{example-normalization-and-structuring}{%
\subsubsection{I. EXAMPLE NORMALIZATION AND STRUCTURING}\label{example-normalization-and-structuring}}

First, we transform a \textbf{natural language query} into a standardized format while removing unnecessary details. This ensures consistency and adaptability across different database queries. Moreover, we analyze the pair of (\textbf{natural language query, SQL code}) to extract the entities that are connected with this request and identify data sources and operations.

This process is executed using a \textbf{Large Language Model (LLM)}, which simultaneously performs the following tasks:

$$
(\mathcal N_k, \mathcal E_k, \mathcal V_k, \mathcal O_k, \mathcal E_k)  = \text{LLM}(\mathcal I_k, \mathcal S_{code_k})
$$

The following rules define how the LLM transforms and extracts structured information from natural language inputs.

\textbf{1. Normalize the Question ($\mathcal N_k$)}

The LLM must standardize the user query by removing unnecessary details and ensuring a structured format. The normalization process follows these steps:

\begin{itemize}
\item
  Remove database-specific names (e.g., table names, column names).
\item
  Remove display instructions (e.g., "show", "display", "generate chart").
\item
  Generalize conditions (e.g., "in 2023" → "for some time range").
\item
  Abstract filtering details (e.g., replace specific IDs, names, or numbers with generalized terms).
\item
  Maintain the directive form (e.g., "Show sales data" → "Sales data").
\end{itemize}

\textbf{2. Requested Entities  ($\mathcal E_k$)}

The LLM identifies \textbf{key data elements} referenced in the query. These entities define the core information being retrieved.

\begin{itemize}
\item
  Identify \textbf{core data} being requested (e.g., "number of transactions per region").
\item
  Keep significant \textbf{categorical definitions} (e.g., "mobile applications", "email campaigns").
\item
  Strip out time filters and specific constraints if they don't affect the entity's identity.
\end{itemize}

Finally, we receive a structured set of entities:

$\mathcal E_k = \{ (L_i, K_i, J_i) \}$ , where $L_i$ — the \textbf{label} of the entity; $K_i$  — the \textbf{type} of entity (category, metric, or identifier); $J_i$ — any \textbf{additional attributes} that provide context.

\textbf{3. Data Sources  ($\mathcal{V_k}$)}

The LLM must determine \textbf{which tables and columns} provide the necessary data.

\begin{itemize}
\item
  If an SQL snippet is provided, extract table names and necessary columns from it.
\item
  If no SQL code is given, keep the data source field empty to avoid assumptions.
\end{itemize}

The structured output for data sources is represented as:

$\mathcal V_k = \{T_{ik},\bold C_{ik}\}$

\textbf{4. Extract Calculations \& Filters}

The LLM must identify and structure any \textbf{mathematical operations, grouping fields, or filtering conditions}.

\begin{itemize}
\item
  Identify aggregation operations (e.g., \texttt{SUM}, \texttt{AVG}, \texttt{COUNT}).
\item
  Extract grouping fields (e.g., \texttt{BY department}).
\item
  Convert all filters and conditions into human-readable form (e.g., \texttt{"where region = 'US'"} → \texttt{"Filter by region (United States)"}).
\end{itemize}

The structured output for operations is represented as:

$\mathcal{O} = \{ (\text{operation}, \text{target}, \text{condition}) \}$

\hypertarget{extracting-the-main-clause-details}{%
\subsubsection{II. EXTRACTING THE MAIN CLAUSE \& DETAILS}\label{extracting-the-main-clause-details}}

Additionally, we expanded the example description with isolated \textbf{request's core} from \textbf{additional details} by using Multi-Shot Learning. This helps structure the SQL query effectively.

The LLM processes the input query by splitting it into two distinct components:

$\mathcal I_k \Rightarrow (\mathcal M_k, \mathcal M'_k)$

where:

\begin{itemize}
\item
  $\mathcal{M}_k$ — The \textbf{Main Clause}, representing the \textbf{primary data request}. Retains the essential meaning of the query. Non-critical modifiers such as filters, grouping, and sorting are excluded.
\item
  $\mathcal M'_k$ — The \textbf{Details} that expand the \textbf{Main Clause}, containing contextual refinements and constraints. Retains \textbf{all auxiliary conditions} without altering the core request. Grouping, filtering, and ordering constraints are explicitly preserved.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Example Transformations:}

\begin{longtable}{p{0.4\textwidth}p{0.25\textwidth}p{0.25\textwidth}}
\toprule
User Query & Extracted Main Clause & Extracted Details \\
\midrule
\endhead
"Show me a list of sales transactions for Q4, including product details and revenue" & "list of sales transactions" & "for Q4, including product details and revenue" \\
"Display the number of active users by country over the past month" & "number of active users" & "by country over the past month" \\
"Get the total revenue for each department last year" & "total revenue for each department" & "over a defined timeframe" \\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

\hypertarget{enrichment-of-the-example-with-the-variation-of-question}{%
\subsubsection{III. ENRICHMENT OF THE EXAMPLE WITH THE VARIATION OF QUESTION}\label{enrichment-of-the-example-with-the-variation-of-question}}

In order to increase the number of embeddings that cover an example, we generate questions that are similar to the normalized initial question, considering SQL code as an answer.

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=4in,height=2.51563in]{images/image_3.png}

\emph{Process of generating variations of questions to enrich examples.}
\end{center}


Let's consider the example:

\begin{longtable}{p{2cm}p{\dimexpr\linewidth-2.5cm\relax}}
\toprule
\textbf{Question} & Show me a list of events in the past month including name, permalink, start date, end date, and RSVP count \\
\midrule
\textbf{SQL Code} & 
\begin{tcolorbox}[
  colback=gray!10,
  colframe=white,
  arc=0mm,
  boxrule=0pt,
  leftrule=0pt,
  rightrule=0pt,
  toprule=0pt,
  bottomrule=0pt
]
\begin{lstlisting}[style=sqlcode]
SELECT title AS name, permalink, start_at AS start_date, 
       end_at AS end_date, rsvp_count
FROM events
WHERE
  start_at >= DATEADD(month, -1, GETDATE())
  AND start_at < GETDATE();
\end{lstlisting}
\end{tcolorbox} \\
\midrule
\textbf{Normalized} & \texttt{"Retrieve a list of events over a defined timeframe including their name, permalink, start date, end date, and RSVP count"} \\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}


The initial question was transformed to the normalized form \textbf{\texttt{"Retrieve a list of events over a defined timeframe including their name, permalink, start date, end date, and RSVP count"}}, which

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{contains} all entities that has to be retrieved
\item
  \textbf{contains} identifier that the request contains some specific timeframe reference
\item
  \textbf{does not contain} any specific details that contradict to the generalisation's principals
\end{itemize}

The requested entity and main clause are \textbf{\texttt{"list of events with their characteristics"}} and \textbf{\texttt{"list of events in the past month"}} correspondingly. In order to provide the wider coverage, we generated the list of three additional questions similar to the normalized representation of the request:

\begin{itemize}
\item
  Provide a list of events occurring within a specified time range, including their names, permalinks, start dates, end dates, and the number of RSVPs.
\item
  Can you give me the details of events within a certain period, listing their name, permalink, start date, end date, and RSVP count?
\item
  Generate a report of events within a certain period, showing their name, permalink, start date, end date, and number of RSVPs
\end{itemize}

So, the example will be described via seven embeddings that increase the invariance to the request's specific when the user asks about the same concepts.

Let's look at the diagrams below. The left diagram represents \textbf{PCA Visualization of Text Embeddings} for two examples. Each color-coded group relates to an example, which is represented via embeddings that originate from an initial question. Each labeled point corresponds to a text embedding, with names such as "MainClause", "Entity", "Normalized", "Similar 1", "Similar 2", "Similar 3", and "Init." We may see that these groups are well distinguished - embeddings within the same group (same example) tend to be closer together.

The diagram on the right represents the inner-cluster pairwise distance comparison \textbf{before and after} adding similar questions. It provides insight into how the embeddings' spread changed with the new data. According to the provided box plots, we may see that \textbf{before adding similar questions}, the embeddings were more compact, meaning that questions and their variations were closely related to the embedding space. \textbf{After adding similar questions}, the increase in distances suggests that the embeddings became more dispersed, possibly introducing more variety and reducing redundancy in the representations.

