\section{Related Work}

Improving the inference efficiency of large language has been extensively studied from multiple perspectives. Quantization techniques such as GPTQ~\cite{frantar2022gptq}, AWQ~\cite{lin2024awq}, SmoothQuant~\cite{xiao2023smoothquant}, and LLM-QAT~\cite{liu2023llm} focus on reducing computational and memory costs. To address long-context KV-cache management, approaches such as GQA~\cite{ainslie2023gqa}, MLA~\cite{liu2024deepseek}, StreamingLLM~\cite{xiao2023efficient}, H2O~\cite{zhang2023h2o}, MoBA~\cite{lu2025moba}, and NSA~\cite{yuan2025native} explicitly balances GPU memory consumption against model accuracy. At the system level, innovations like FlashAttention~\cite{dao2022flashattention}, FlashDecoding~\cite{hong2023flashdecoding++}, MEGABLOCKS~\cite{gale2023megablocks}, and vLLM~\cite{kwon2023efficient} deliver optimized kernels and scheduling strategies to maximize hardware utilization and throughput.

Speculative decoding~\cite{leviathan2023fast}~\cite{chen2023accelerating} improve GPU parallelism by leveraging a draft model to generate candidate tokens, which are then verified by the target model, achieving speedup without compromising accuracy. Other approaches such as LOOKAHEAD~\cite{fu2024break}, PLD+~\cite{somasundaram2024pld+}, REST~\cite{he2023rest} and SuffixDecoding~\cite{oliaro2024suffixdecoding} utilize text-based retrieval mechanisms to generate more informed drafts. LayerSkip~\cite{Elhoushi_2024}, Kangaroo~\cite{liu2024kangaroo}, and SWIFT~\cite{xia2024swift} reuse selected layers of the target model to construct a lightweight draft model.

To improve the accuracy of speculative decoding, methods like Medusa~\cite{cai2024medusa}, EAGLE~\cite{li2024eagle}, Amphista~\cite{li_amphista_2024}, Jakiro~\cite{huang2025jakiro} and Hydra~\cite{ankner2024hydra} incorporate representations from the target model as additional input signals. Approaches such as BiTA~\cite{BiTA_2024}, ParallelSpec~\cite{Xiao2024}, and PaSS~\cite{monea2023pass} introduce mask tokens to enable parallel speculative decoding. Further, techniques including Spectr~\cite{sun2023spectr}, SpecInfer~\cite{miao2023specinfer}, Sequoia~\cite{chen2024sequoia}, and EAGLE-2~\cite{li2024eagle} optimize tree-based verification structures to enhance token acceptance rates.


\section{Conclusion}

In this work, we propose PARD, a novel speculative decoding framework that adapts the vanilla draft model into a parallel mode, significantly accelerating autoregressive generation while maintaining vanilla draft model accuracy. We introduce a conditional drop token strategy that speeds up training by over \textbf{3$\times$}. Our experiments demonstrate that PARD achieves a maximum \textbf{5.38$\times$} speedup over optimized autoregressive decoding and an average \textbf{1.78$\times$} improvement over vanilla speculative decoding. For Qwen2.5-7B, PARD reaches a peak throughput of \textbf{381} tokens per second on a single A100-40GB GPU. Additionally, PARD exhibits strong target independence, providing consistent acceleration across various model families, including LLaMA3, DeepSeek-R1, and Qwen. PARD establishes itself as a practical and effective approach for high-performance language model inference.


