In this paper, we restrict the attention to rectilinear polygons, partition them into rectangles, and for the stabbing number only consider line segments that are in the interior of the polygon and axis-aligned (we call these \emph{stabbing segments}).
More precisely, we study the following problem for a rectilinear $ n $-gon $ P $, possibly with holes:  partition $ P $ into rectangles while minimizing the \emph{stabbing number of the partition}, 
that is, the maximum over all stabbing segments $ \mathbf { s } $ of the  number of partition rectangles intersected by $ \mathbf { s } $. 
We often describe such a partition via the inserted segments. 
A \emph{Steiner point} of a partition is an endpoint of a segment that does not lie on $ P $.
We say that such a rectangular partition is \emph{optimal}, and call its stabbing number the \emph{(minimum) stabbing number of $ P $}. 
Figure~\ref{fig:partitionWithSteinerPointsVSConforming}(a) shows an example of an optimal partition.
Similar to triangular partitions, every rectilinear polygon has stabbing number $ \OO ( \log n ) $, and there exist polygons of arbitrary size with stabbing number $ \Omega ( \log n ) $~\cite{DV94}. %
However, there also exist arbitrary-size polygons with stabbing number $ \OO ( 1 ) $. 
To this end, Abam et al.~\cite{AADK11} gave a $ 3 $-approximation algorithm for computing the stabbing number of simple rectilinear polygons. 
An interesting open problem in this context is to determine the computational complexity of computing the stabbing number for simple polygons. 
Although this question remains open in general, there has been some progress on a variant of rectangular partition called conforming partition. 


A \emph{conforming partition} of a rectilinear polygon $ P $ is a rectangular partition without Steiner points. 
Put differently, the partition is obtained by using internally disjoint axis-aligned segments that are \emph{maximal} (i.e., both endpoints are in $ P $).  
To minimize the stabbing number, it suffices to restrict the attention to partitions that use only \emph{reflex segments}, i.e., maximal axis-aligned open segments where one endpoint is a reflex vertex of $ P $ (Figure~\ref{fig:partitionWithSteinerPointsVSConforming}(b)). 
Again, we say that a conforming partition is \emph{optimal} if its stabbing number is minimum among all the conforming partitions, and we call this stabbing number the \emph{conforming stabbing number of $ P $}.
Durocher and Mehhrabi~\cite{DM17,DM17erratum} showed that computing an optimal conforming partition is $ \NP $-hard for polygons with holes,
and gave a $ 2 $-approximation algorithm for computing the conforming stabbing number (see also~\cite{piva2017minimum} for experimental results). 
However, the complexity of the problem remains open for simple polygons without holes.


\paragraph{Contributions.} In this paper, we investigate the problem of computing an optimal conforming partition of rectilinear polygons (possibly with holes) from the perspective of designing \emph{fixed-parameter tractable (FPT) algorithms}, i.e., algorithms with a running time of the form $ f ( k ) n ^ { \OO ( 1 ) } $ for some chosen parameter $ k $ and some computable function $ f ( \cdot ) $  that is independent of $ n $. 
A natural question in the context of asking for (conforming) partitions with stabbing number at most $ k $ is to search for an FPT algorithm parameterized by $ k $. 
We show that such an algorithm does not exist unless $ \PP = \NP $. 
Specifically, deciding whether the conforming stabbing number (and in fact the stabbing number) of a polygon is at most~$ 4 $ remains $ \NP $-hard (Section~\ref{sec:4+intratable}). 
This strengthens the $ \NP $-hardness result of Durocher and Mehhrabi~\cite{DM17}, who show it is $ \NP $-hard to determine whether the conforming stabbing number is $ \Theta ( \sqrt { n } ) $. 


Our hardness result puts forward two interesting questions. 
First, is it decidable whether a rectilinear polygon admits a conforming partition with stabbing number at most~$ 2 $ or~$ 3 $ in polynomial time? 
Second, are there other natural parameters for FPT algorithms to compute optimal conforming partitions?
For the former, we give an $ \OO ( n \log n ) $-time algorithm to decide whether a conforming partition with stabbing number~$ 2 $ exists (Section~\ref{sec:2tractable}); this leaves the case of stabbing number~$ 3 $ open.
For the latter, we give two FPT algorithms to test whether a polygon $ P $ has conforming stabbing number at most $ k $ (Section~\ref{sec:tw}). 
One is parameterized by the sum of $ k $ plus the \emph{treewidth} of $ P $, the other is specific to simple polygons in \emph{general position} and is parameterized by $ k $ alone. 









\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:preliminaries}




Throughout the article, the polygons we consider are all rectilinear (i.e., the edges are axis-aligned) and may contain holes.
A polygon is in \emph{general position} if no three vertices lie on one axis-aligned line (Figure~\ref{fig:partitionWithSteinerPointsVSConforming}(c)). 
A polygon is \emph{thin} if no pair of its reflex segments intersect (Figure~\ref{fig:partitionWithSteinerPointsVSConforming}(d)-(e)). 


The \emph{pixelation} of a polygon $ P $ (possibly with holes) is the partition of $ P $ obtained by adding for each reflex vertex $ p $ its horizontal and vertical reflex segments; these segments are denoted $ \mathbf { h } _ p $ and $ \mathbf { v } _ p $ (Figure~\ref{fig:partitionWithSteinerPointsVSConforming}(c)-(d)). 
A \emph{pixel} is a maximal region of $ P $ that does not intersect a reflex segment.  
For a reflex vertex $ p $ of $ P $, the \emph{wedge-pixel of $ p $} is the pixel incident to the wedge defined by the reflex segments of $ p $, i.e., the pixel that is incident to $ p $ and to $ \mathbf { h } _ p $ and $ \mathbf { v } _ p $ (Figure~\ref{fig:partitionWithSteinerPointsVSConforming}(c)). 


Recall that a \emph{stabbing segment} of a rectilinear polygon $ P $ is an axis-aligned line segment that lies in the interior of $ P $; for purposes of the stabbing number we only need to consider segments of maximal length, and we consider them to be open segments.
We say that two stabbing segments are \emph{equivalent} if they intersect the same set of pixels; there are $ \OO ( n ) $ equivalence classes of stabbing segments.
For instance, in Figure~\ref{fig:partitionWithSteinerPointsVSConforming}(c), there are $ 26 $ equivalence classes. 
Given a rectilinear polygon $ P $, by  $ \STAB { k } $ ($ \CSTAB { k } $) we denote the problem of deciding whether $ P $ admits a partition (conforming partition) into rectangles such that all stabbing segments intersect at most $ k $ rectangles. 


We will reduce from an $ \NP $-hard problem called \emph{rectilinear planar monotone $ \SAT { 3 } $} ($ \RPM $)~\cite{BK12} to prove the hardness results.
The $ \RPM $ problem is a variant of $ \SAT { 3 } $ where every clause is either negative or positive, i.e., consists of either three negated or three non-negated variables. 
Furthermore, the bipartite graph constructed from the variable-clause incidences admits a planar drawing such that all vertices are drawn as rectangles, the \emph{variable rectangles} (i.e., rectangles of vertices corresponding to variables) lie along the $ x $-axis, the \emph{positive (negative) clause rectangles} (i.e., rectangles of vertices corresponding to such clauses) lie above (below) the $ x $-axis, and edges are represented by vertical lines of visibility between the rectangles of their endpoints. 
Figure~\ref{fig:fullGadgetStabbingN4}(a) illustrates such an instance where the rectangles are shaded in gray. 












\section{Intractability of Stabbing Number \texorpdfstring{$ 4 $}{4} or More}\label{sec:4+intratable}


In this section, we sketch a proof of $ \NP $-completeness; details are in Appendix~\ref{sec:intractableProof}.


\begin{theorem}\label{thm:NPHardStabbingN4+}
    For all integer $ k \geq 4 $, the decision problems $ \STAB { k } $ and $ \CSTAB { k } $ are $ \NP $-complete. 
    Moreover, $ \CSTAB { k } $ remains $ \NP $-complete even for thin polygons and for polygons in general position.
\end{theorem}


\paragraph{Proof structure.}
It is straightforward to verify that $ \STAB { k } $ and $ \CSTAB { k } $ are in $ \NP $ for any integer $ k $.
We thus concentrate on proving $ \NP $-hardness. 
First, we prove that $ \CSTAB { 4 } $ is $ \NP $-hard, even if only thin polygons are considered. 
In a thin polygon, any optimal partition is conforming, so in consequence $ \STAB { 4 } $ is also $ \NP $-hard. 
However, the gadgets take advantage of not being in general position.
Second, we provide an alternative version of this proof, this time for polygons in general position (but the gadgets take advantage of not being thin).
As an aside, we note here that it is not possible to make the gadgets both thin and in general position; the problem is actually polynomial in this case, see Theorem~\ref{thm:thinGeneral}.



As a third step, using a similar approach and with a similar alternative version, we prove that $ \CSTAB { 5 } $, and thus $ \STAB { 5 } $, are $ \NP $-hard. 
Finally, we show how to modify our constructions for $ \CSTAB { \ell } $ ($ \ell \in \{ 4 , 5 \} $) to work for $ \CSTAB { \ell + 2 m } $ for any $ m \geq 1 $.   Therefore $ \CSTAB { k } $ is $ \NP $-hard for thin polygons for all $ k \geq 4 $, which implies hardness for $ \STAB { k } $.


\paragraph{Proof sketch of the $ \NP $-hardness of $ \CSTAB { 4 } $ for thin polygons.}
We reduce $ \RPM $ (defined in the preliminaries) to $ \STAB { 4 } $ in polynomial time.
We transform an instance $ \formula $ of $ \RPM $ (shaded in the background of Figure~\ref{fig:fullGadgetStabbingN4}(a)) into an instance $ P ( \formula ) $ of $ \STAB { 4 } $ (the polygon in Figure~\ref{fig:fullGadgetStabbingN4}(a)).





The polygon $ P ( \formula ) $ consists of \emph{variable gadgets} (drawn inside the variable rectangles), \emph{split gadgets} (drawn above and/or below the variable gadgets and still inside the variable rectangles), and \emph{clause gadgets} (drawn inside the clause rectangles). 
Crucial to our construction are \emph{forcer gadgets}, indicated by a square labeled F in Figure~\ref{fig:fullGadgetStabbingN4}(a) and shown in detail in Figure~\ref{fig:fullGadgetStabbingN4}(b).
A forcer gadget is designed to force the presence (in any conforming partition with stabbing number at most~$ 4 $) of a certain pair of reflex segments in the pixel to which it is attached.


