Recall that our approach is to apply the above rules, and to keep track (by storing them in two lists $ \Lfixed $ and $ \Limpossible $) of all reflex segments that we determine to be fixed or impossible.   
(There may be other fixed or impossible segments that we do not find.)
This clearly can be done in polynomial time; we show in Appendix~\ref{sec:runtime} how to implement it in $ \OO ( n \log n ) $ time by applying line-sweep and ray-shooting techniques.
If some segment $ \mathbf { s } $ belongs to both $ \Lfixed $ and $ \Limpossible $, then we conclude that there is no conforming partition with stabbing number~$ 2 $.


We are left with three possible outcomes:
We find that there is no conforming partition with stabbing number at most~$ 2 $, or $ \Lfixed $ defines a conforming partition, or neither.
We are done in the first outcome.
We are also done in the second outcome: 
If $ \Lfixed $ defines a conforming partition, then by (R3) (and since $ \Lfixed $ is disjoint from $ \Limpossible $ ) every stabbing segment intersects at most one segment of $ \Lfixed $, and so the stabbing number is~$ 2 $.  
In the third outcome, we provide an algorithm to test in linear time whether there exists a solution.
(In fact, there \emph{always} is a solution, but for space reasons we do not prove this.) 


Add the segments of $ \Lfixed $ into $ P $ to obtain a partition of $ P $ into rectilinear polygons $ P _ 1 , \dots , P _ \ell $ that we call the \emph{pieces} of $ P $.
The idea is now to solve the problem for each piece of $ P $ and to put the solutions together. 
Next, we make two useful observations.


\begin{observation}\label{obs:rflxSgtOfPieceIsRflxSgtOfP}
    For every piece $ P _ i $, every reflex segment $ \mathbf { s } $ of $ P _ i $ is a
    reflex segment of $ P $ that was undecided (i.e., neither in $ \Lfixed $ nor in $ \Limpossible $).
\end{observation}


\begin{proof}
Since $ \mathbf { s } $ is a reflex segment of $ P _ i $, one endpoint of $ \mathbf { s } $ is a reflex vertex of $ P _ i $, hence also a reflex vertex of $ P $.
The other endpoint of $ \mathbf { s } $ lies on the boundary of $ P _ i $. 
If this other endpoint were not on $ P $, then it would be on the interior of a segment $ \mathbf { s } ' \in \Lfixed $. 
But then rule (R4) would have been applied to $ \mathbf { s ' } $ and the reflex segment of $ P $ containing $ \mathbf { s } $.
This would have added $ \mathbf { s }'$ to $ \Limpossible $, contradicting that $ \Lfixed $ and $ \Limpossible $ are disjoint.
Thus the other endpoint of $ \mathbf { s } $ also lies on the boundary of $ P $, and $ \mathbf { s } $ is a reflex segment of $ P $.


To see that $ \mathbf { s } $ is undecided, observe first that $ p $ does not have an incident reflex segment in $ \Lfixed $ since it is reflex in the piece $ P _ i $.   Thus $ \mathbf { s } \not \in \Lfixed $, and also $ \mathbf { s } \not \in \Limpossible $, since otherwise rule (R2) would have added the other reflex segment at $ p $ to $ \Lfixed $. 
Therefore $ \mathbf { s } $ is undecided.
\end{proof}


\begin{observation}
    \label{obs:solvePieces}
    $ P $ has a solution to $ \CSTAB { 2 } $ if and only if each of the pieces $ P _ 1 , \dots , P _ \ell $ of $ P $ has a solution to $ \CSTAB { 2 } $.
\end{observation}


\begin{proof}
Any solution for $ P $ includes all segments of $ \Lfixed $, thereby yielding a solution for each piece.
Vice versa, assume that each piece $ P _ i $ of $ P $ admits a solution $ \mathcal{R} _ i $ of reflex segments to $ \CSTAB { 2 } $. 
We show that $ \mathcal { R } : = \Lfixed \cup \bigcup _ i \mathcal { R } _ i $ is a solution for $ P $.
To see that $ \mathcal { R } $ is a conforming partition, observe that it only contains reflex segments of $ P $ by Observation~\ref{obs:rflxSgtOfPieceIsRflxSgtOfP}, and assigns at least one reflex segment to each reflex vertex of $ P $.
Since the pieces $ P _ 1 , \dots , P _ \ell $ are interior-disjoint, the reflex segments in $ \bigcup _ i \mathcal { R } _ i $ do not intersect each other. They do not intersect a segment of $ \Lfixed $ either, by Observation~\ref{obs:rflxSgtOfPieceIsRflxSgtOfP}, so $ \mathcal { R } $ yields a conforming partition.


To show that $ \mathcal { R } $ has stabbing number at most~$ 2 $, consider any stabbing segment $ \mathbf { s } $ of $ P $. 
If $ \mathbf { s } $ intersects no segment of $ \Lfixed $, then it is also a stabbing segment for one piece $ P _ i $, and so will intersect at most one segment of $ \mathcal { R } $.
Now assume that $ \mathbf { s } $ intersects a segment of $ \Lfixed $.
Since rule (R3) was applied, all other reflex segments of $ P $ intersected by $ \mathbf { s } $ were added to $ \Limpossible $, so were not reflex segments of any pieces, and hence are not used by $ \mathcal { R } $.
Therefore, stabbing segment $ \mathbf { s } $ intersects at most one segment of $ \mathcal { R } $.
\end{proof}


It remains to show how to solve the problem for each piece efficiently.
Here, our previous  $ \SAT { 2 } $ approach comes to the rescue since the pieces are not arbitrary polygons.
Specifically, since rule (R4) does not apply to piece $ P _ i $ (for $ i \in \{ 1 , \dots , \ell \} $), it has no intersecting reflex segments, so it is thin. 
Since rule (R5) does not apply to $ P _ i $, it has no gate.
We now prove a statement that holds for any thin gate-free polygon.


\begin{lemma}
    \label{lem:thinGeneral}
    Let $ P $ be a thin rectilinear polygon that has no gate. 
    Then every stabbing segment $ \mathbf { s } $ intersects at most two reflex segments. 
\end{lemma}


\begin{proof}
Assume for contradiction that $ \mathbf { s } $ intersects three reflex segments, say $ \mathbf { s } $ intersects $ \mathbf { s } _ 1 , \mathbf { s } _ 2 , \mathbf { s } _ 3 $, in this order and with no other reflex segments in between. 
See also Figure~\ref{fig:thinGeneral}.   
Up to symmetry $ \mathbf { s } $ is horizontal, so $ \mathbf { s _ 1 } , \mathbf { s } _ 2 , \mathbf { s _ 3 } $ are vertical, and up to renaming $ \mathbf { s _ 1 } $ is on the left of $ \mathbf { s _ 2 } $.
Let $ p $ be the reflex vertex of $ P $ with $ \mathbf { s } _ 2 = \mathbf { v } _ p $. 


Up to symmetry, the wedge-pixel $ \pixel $ of $ p $ is to the left of $ \mathbf { v } _ p $ and below $ \mathbf { h } _ p $.
Since $ P $ is thin, pixel $ \pixel $ extends the entire length of $ \mathbf { v } _ p $, and in particular includes the point common to $ \mathbf { s } $ and $ \mathbf { v } _ p $.
Since there are no vertical reflex segments between $ \mathbf { s } _ 1 $ and $ \mathbf { s } _ 2 $ along $ \mathbf { s } $, pixel $ \pixel $ extends to the point common to $ \mathbf { s } _ 1 $ and $ \mathbf { s } $, and therefore the entire length of $ \mathbf { s } _ 1 $. 
It also includes the entire length of $ \mathbf { h } _ p $. 
Thus the top left corner of $ \pixel $ is a point $ q $ common to $ \mathbf { s } _ 1 $ and $ \mathbf { h } _ p $, hence $ q $ is a reflex vertex that lies on a horizontal line with $ p $. 
Since both $ \mathbf { v } _ q $ and $ \mathbf { v } _ q $  bound sides of $ \pixel $, this makes $ \overline { p q } $ a gate.
\end{proof}


\begin{figure}[hpt]
    \hspace*{\fill}
    \includegraphics[scale=\graphicsScale,page=7]{graphics/rules.pdf}
    \hspace*{\fill}
    \caption{If stabbing segment $ \mathbf { s } $ intersects three reflex segments, and no two reflex segments intersect, then the polygon is not in general position.   The upper left corner of the wedge-pixel $ \pixel $ of $ p $ (and $ q $) is shaded (in orange).}
    \label{fig:thinGeneral}
\end{figure}


Lemma~\ref{lem:thinGeneral} has two consequences:


\begin{theorem}
    \label{thm:thinGeneral}
    There exists an algorithm that, for a rectilinear $ n $-gon $ P $ that is thin and in general position, computes the stabbing number in $ \OO ( n ) $ time.
\end{theorem}


\begin{proof}
The stabbing number of $ P $ is~$ 1 $ if $ P $ is a rectangle, and at least~$ 2 $ otherwise. 
A polygon in general position has no gates, hence any stabbing segment of $ P $ intersects at most three reflex segments by Lemma~\ref{lem:thinGeneral}. 
So the stabbing number of $ P $ (which is equal to the conforming stabbing number since $ P $ is thin) is either~$ 2 $ or~$ 3 $. 
Lemma~\ref{lem:2SAT} gives an algorithm to test whether it is~$ 2 $ in $ \OO ( n ) $ time. 
\end{proof}


\begin{theorem}
    There exists an algorithm that, for any rectilinear $ n $-gon $ P $, decides $ \CSTAB { 2 } $  and provides a solution (if any) in $ \OO ( n \log n ) $ time.
\end{theorem}


\begin{proof}
Apply all rules; this takes $ \OO ( n \log n ) $ time.  
Then compute the pieces $ P _ 1 , \dots , P _ \ell $ in $ \OO ( n ) $ time. 
Using Lemma~\ref{lem:thinGeneral} and Theorem~\ref{thm:thinGeneral}, test in $ \OO ( | P _ i | ) $ time whether a piece $ P _ i $ has a solution for $ \CSTAB { 2 } $. 
By Observation~\ref{obs:solvePieces}, this information is enough to decide $ \CSTAB { 2 } $ for $ P $ and compute the solution in case of an affirmative answer.
Since $ \sum _ i | P _ i | \in \OO ( n ) $, the result follows. 
\end{proof}










\section{Polygons with Small Treewidth}\label{sec:tw}


We now turn towards FPT algorithms, and in particular, study polygons with bounded treewidth. 
We recall first a few definitions.
A \emph{tree decomposition} of a graph $G = (V, E)$ is a tree $ \mathcal{T}$ and an assignment $ \beta$ from the nodes of $ \mathcal{T}$ to subsets of $V$ 
(called \emph{bags}) with the following properties: 
(a) For every vertex $v$ of $G$, the bags containing $v$ form a non-empty connected subtree of $ \mathcal{T}$. 
(b) For every edge $e$ of $G$, there exists a bag that contains both endpoints of $e$.
The \emph{width} of a tree decomposition is the maximum bag-size minus one, and the \emph{treewidth} $ \tw(G)$ of $G$ is the minimum width of a tree decomposition of $G$. 


The treewidth has frequently been used for FPT algorithms for graph problems, but can also be used for solving problems on polygons, see e.g.~\cite{BiedlMehrabi}.
Recall that the \emph{pixelation} of a polygon $ P $ is obtained by inserting all reflex segments.

This gives rise to a planar graph (the \emph{pixelation graph} $G_P$ by replacing every crossing with a new vertex of degree~$ 4 $ and every endpoint of a reflex segment on an edge of $ P $ with a vertex of degree~$ 3 $ (see Figure~\ref{fig:MSOL_ex}(a)). 
The \emph{treewidth} of $ P $ is the treewidth $ \tw(G_P)$ of the pixelation graph.


Our algorithm for polygons with small treewidth uses not only the pixelation graph, but also its \emph{radial graph} $ R _ P $ and defined as follows.  
The vertices of $ R _ P $ are the vertices of $G_P$ (we denote them by $ V _ P $), as well as one vertex for every pixel (we denote these by $ \pixelset_P$). 
We add an edge $ (\pixel,v)$ between $ \pixel\in \pixelset_P$ and $v\in V_P$ if and only if vertex $v$ is incident to pixel $ \pixel $.   
See Figure~\ref{fig:MSOL_ex}(b).
Using the techniques of Borradaile et al.~\cite{BorradaileELW17}, one can easily show that $ R _ P $ has treewidth $ \OO(\tw(G_P))$, since pixels are incident to four vertices of $G_P$.

