\section{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:NPHardStabbingN4+}}\label{sec:intractableProof}


In this section, we provide a detailed proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:NPHardStabbingN4+}.


The following definition is used to shorten the writing of some frequent terms in the proofs.
The vertical (respectively horizontal) reflex segments of $ P $ on the boundary of a pixel $ \pixel $ are called the \emph{verticals} (respectively \emph{horizontals}) of $ \pixel $.
We also use the following lemmas.


\begin{lemma}\label{lem:consequences(thin)}
    The stabbing number of a thin polygon (possibly with holes) $ P $ is equal to the conforming stabbing number of $ P $.
\end{lemma}


\begin{proof}
    Let $ \mathcal{R} $ be a rectangular partition of $ P $ with a Steiner point $ q $. We show that removing $ q $ and merging some adjacent rectangles yields a rectangular partition of $ P $.  


    By definition of a thin polygon, $ q $ is not the intersection of two reflex segments of $ P $.
    Thus, $ q $ is adjacent to at least one segment $ r $ of $ \mathcal{R} $ which is not a reflex segment of $ P $.
    Now, $ r $ is necessarily a full edge (and not only portions of an edge) shared by two rectangles of $ \mathcal{R} $, because otherwise, $ P $ cannot be thin. 
    Therefore, merging these two rectangles yields a rectangle. Repeating this process for all segments $ r $ eventually removes the $ q $ from the partition but does not increase the stabbing number of the partition.
\end{proof}


\begin{lemma}\label{lem:consequences(pixel)}
    Let $ P $ be a polygon and $ \pixel $ be a pixel of $ P $ such that $ \pixel $ is the wedge-pixel of any of its corners that is a reflex segment of $ P $.
    Then a minimal conforming partition of $ P $ either includes the vertical reflex segments of $ \pixel $ (and excludes the horizontal reflex segments of $ \pixel $), or includes the horizontal reflex segments of $ p $ (and excludes the vertical reflex segments of $ \pixel $). 
\end{lemma}


\begin{proof}
    If $k=2$, then there is only one reflex vertex. Hence the lemma is a direct consequence of the definition of a minimal conforming partition.


    If $ k = 3 $, then  $ \pixel $ has two reflex vertices $r,r'$ on the boundary that are adjacent. If we take the reflex segment between $r,r'$, then we do not need the remaining two reflex segments as the partition is minimal. If we do not take the reflex segment between $r,r'$, then to cover these reflex vertices, we must take the two other parallel reflex segments. 


    If $ k = 4 $, then there are four reflex vertices on the boundary of $ \pixel $. There are two ways to cover the four reflex vertices using two reflex segments: either to take the horizontals or the verticals. If we use three reflex segments, then at least one of them would be unnecessary for a minimal conforming partition.
\end{proof} 








\subsection{The Problem \texorpdfstring{$ \RPM $}{RPM-3SAT} to Be Reduced}


Before defining problem \RPM, we recall some terminology.
The \emph{graph} of a $ 3 $-CNF formula $ \formula $ is the undirected bipartite graph $G_\formula $ defined as follows.
The vertex set is the disjoint union of the set of variables of $ \formula $ and of the set of clauses of $ \formula $.
There is an edge between a variable $ x $ and a clause $ c $ if $ x $ is a variable of $ c $ in $ \formula $.


\begin{figure}[hpt]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=\graphicsScale,page=1]{graphics/fullGadgetStabbingN4.pdf}%
    \caption{A $ \RPM $ drawing of $ \formula = (x_1 \lor x_3 \lor x_4) \land  (x_1 \lor x_2 \lor x_3) \land  (\overline {x_1} \lor \overline {x_2} \lor \overline {x_4})$.}
    \label{fig:RPMStabbingN4}
\end{figure}


If a $ 3 $-CNF formula $ \formula $ is \emph{monotone}, that is to say, if any clause of $ \formula $ contains either three positive literals or three negative literals, then a drawing $ D $ of the graph of $ \formula $ is \emph{rectilinear planar monotone} (or \emph{RPM}) if the following holds (see e.g., Figure~\ref{fig:RPMStabbingN4}).
\begin{itemize}
    \item The variable vertices are drawn with axis-aligned rectangles centered on the $ x $-axis.
    \item The positive clauses are drawn with axis-aligned rectangles above the $ x $-axis.
    \item The negative clauses are drawn with axis-aligned rectangles below the $ x $-axis.
    \item The edges are drawn with open axis-aligned rectangles between the corresponding variable and clause rectangles. 
    \item All of the variable rectangles, clause rectangles, and edge rectangles are pairwise disjoint.
\end{itemize}


\begin{problem}[\RPM]
    \label{pb:RPM3SATStabbingN4}
    \emph{Input:} A rectilinear planar monotone drawing of a $ 3 $-CNF formula $ \formula $. 
    \emph{Output:} Accept if $ \formula $ is satisfiable. Reject otherwise.
\end{problem}


Problem~\ref{pb:RPM3SATStabbingN4} is known to be $ \NP $-hard~\cite{BK12}.







\subsection{The Forcer Gadget for \texorpdfstring{$ k = 4 $}{k=4} Using Thin Polygons}


\paragraph{Overview.} A \emph{forcer gadget}  (Figure~\ref{fig:forcerGadgetStabbingN4}(a)) is a thin polygon similar to a $4\times 4$ grid, with an extension to attach it to the rest of the polygon through an edge called \emph{connection edge}. This is the only edge that is not drawn in the bootmmost row of Figure~\ref{fig:forcerGadgetStabbingN4}(a). By an  \emph{out-stab} of the force gadget we denote a segment that starts at a boundary point and leaves the forcer gadget after perpendicularly intersecting the connection edge, e.g., the thick segment of Figure~\ref{fig:forcerGadgetStabbingN4}(c) and (d). We now give the formal details.   


