\paragraph{Details of a Clause Gadget.}
Let $ \mya, b, c, d, e $ be positive integers, and $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ be the polygon without holes defined as follows (Figure~\ref{fig:clauseGadgetStabbingN4}).
The coordinates of the vertices of $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ in counterclockwise order along the boundary are:
\begin{align*}
    (& (0, 0), (0, - c - 1), (1, - c - 1), (1, - 1), (\mya + 1, - 1), (\mya + 1, - d - 1), \\
    & (\mya + 2, - d - 1), (\mya + 2, - 1), (\mya + b + 2, - 1), (\mya + b + 2, - e - 1), \\
    & (\mya + b + 3, - e - 1), (\mya + b + 3, 0)).
\end{align*}
An rectilinear polygon $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ is a \emph{clause gadget} in the following two cases.
\begin{itemize}
    \item If there exists a translation $ \tau $ such that $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) = \tau (C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e)) $, then $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ is a called a \emph{positive} clause gadget.
    \item If there exists a transformation $ \tau $ such that $ \tau $ is the composition of a horizontal reflection with a translation and such that $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) = \tau (C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e)) $, then $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ is a called a \emph{negative} clause gadget.
\end{itemize}
Next, we give names to some segments of interest of $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) $.
\begin{itemize}
    \item The edge $ \tau ((0, - c - 1) (1, - c - 1)) $ is the \emph{left connection edge} of $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ (the bottom left segment of the outer boundary of $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ which is not drawn in Figure~\ref{fig:clauseGadgetStabbingN4}).
    \item The edge $ \tau ((\mya + 1, - d - 1) (\mya + 2, - d - 1)) $ is the \emph{center connection edge} of $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ \ (the bottom center segment of the outer boundary of $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ which is not drawn in Figure~\ref{fig:clauseGadgetStabbingN4}).
    \item The edge $ \tau ((\mya + b + 2, - e - 1) (\mya + b + 3, - e - 1)) $ is the \emph{right connection edge} of $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ (the bottom right segment of the outer boundary of $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ which is not drawn in Figure~\ref{fig:clauseGadgetStabbingN4}).
    \item The stabbing segment $ \tau ((0.5, 0) (0.5, - c - 1)) $ is the \emph{left in-stab} of $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ (the leftmost thick segment drawn with an arrow pointing upwards inside $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ in Figure~\ref{fig:clauseGadgetStabbingN4}(b), (c), (d), and (e)).
    \item The stabbing segment $ \tau ((\mya + 1.5, 0) (\mya + 1.5, - d - 1)) $ is the \emph{center in-stab} of $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ (the center thick segment drawn with an arrow pointing upwards inside $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ in Figure~\ref{fig:clauseGadgetStabbingN4}(b), (c), (d), and (e)).
    \item The stabbing segment $ \tau ((\mya + b + 2.5, 0) (\mya + b + 2.5, - e - 1)) $ is the \emph{right in-stab} of $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ (the rightmost thick segment drawn with an arrow pointing upwards inside $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ in Figure~\ref{fig:clauseGadgetStabbingN4}(b), (c), (d), and (e)).
\end{itemize}


\begin{lemma}\label{lem:clauseGadgetStabbingN4}
    Any clause gadget $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ admits exactly~$ 8 $ minimal conforming partitions.
    Specifically, these~$ 8 $ minimal conforming partitions are the $ \mathcal{R}_{x y z} $ such that exactly $ x \in \{0, 1 \} $ (respectively $ y $, $ z $) reflex segments of $ \mathcal{R}_{x y z} $ intersect the left (respectively center, right) in-stab of $ C (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ (Figure~\ref{fig:clauseGadgetStabbingN4}(b), (c), (d), and (e) show respectively $ \mathcal{R}_{0 0 0}, \mathcal{R}_{0 0 1}, \mathcal{R}_{0 1 1}, \mathcal{R}_{1 1 1} $).
    Moreover, only $ \mathcal{R}_{0 0 0} $ has stabbing number greater than~$ 4 $.
\end{lemma}


\begin{proof}
    It is enough to prove Lemma~\ref{lem:clauseGadgetStabbingN4} for $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $.
    We start by naming more parts of $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $.
    The segment $ (0, - 0.5) (\mya + b + 3, - 0.5) $ is the \emph{inner stab} of $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ (the horizontal thick (purple or green) segment in Figure~\ref{fig:clauseGadgetStabbingN4}(b), (c), (d) and (e)).
    The pixel consisting of a unit square is numbered $ k $ if its lower left corner is
    (Figure~\ref{fig:clauseGadgetStabbingN4}(a)):
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $ (0, -1) $ and $ k = 1 $,
        \item $ (\mya + 1, -1) $ and $ k = 2 $, or
        \item $ (\mya + b + 2, -1) $ and $ k = 3 $.
    \end{itemize}


    Lemma~\ref{lem:consequences(pixel)} applied to pixels $ 1, 2, 3 $ indeed shows that there exists exactly~$ 8 $ minimal conforming partition of $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ which are $ \{\mathcal{R}_{x y z} : x, y, z \in \{0, 1 \} \} $ as stated in Lemma~\ref{lem:clauseGadgetStabbingN4}.


    Given that the three pixels $ 1, 2, 3 $ all intersect the inner stab of $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $, we check that the inner stab of $ C_0 (\mya, b, c, d, e) $ intersects at most~$ 4 $ reflex segments of the $ \mathcal{R}_{x y z}$ except $ \mathcal{R}_{0 0 0} $.
\end{proof}







\subsection{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:NPHardStabbingN4+} When \texorpdfstring{$k=4$}{k=4} Using Polygons in General Position}


It is straightforward to observe that $ \STAB { 4 } $ and $ \CSTAB {4} $ are in $ \NP $. We now prove that the problem $ \CSTAB {4} $ is $ \NP $-hard by reducing Problem~\ref{pb:RPM3SATStabbingN4} to $ \CSTAB {4} $ in polynomial time. Our reduction uses thin polygons, and hence by Lemma~\ref{lem:consequences(thin)}, the hardness result holds for $ \STAB { 4 } $.


Let  $ D, \formula $ be an instance of Problem~\ref{pb:RPM3SATStabbingN4}, that is, a rectilinear planar monotone drawing $ D $ of a $ 3 $-CNF formula $ \formula $ ($ D $ is shaded in Figure~\ref{fig:fullGadgetStabbingN4} and displayed alone in Figure~\ref{fig:RPMStabbingN4}). 
Let $ u $ be the number of clauses of $ \formula $ and $v $ be the number of variables of $ \formula $. The number of variable gadgets and clause gadgets is at most $u+v$. The number of split gadgets is at most a constant factor of the number of clause gadgets. Therefore, it is straightforward to construct the corresponding polygon $ P (D, \formula) $, i.e., an instance of the stabbing number problem,  (Figure~\ref{fig:fullGadgetStabbingN4}(a)) in polynomial time in $ u + v $. 


Next, we prove that the formula $ \formula $ is satisfiable if and only if $ P (D, \formula) $ admits a minimal conforming partition with stabbing number at most~$ 4 $.


Assume that the polygon $ P (D, \formula) $ admits a conforming partition with stabbing number at most~$ 4 $. 
By Lemma~\ref{lem:variableGadgetStabbingN4}, each variable gadget of $ P (D, \formula) $ admits exactly three minimal conforming partitions with stabbing number at most~$ 4 $, one standing for true, one standing for false, and the last one being undetermined. Because each out-stab pairs up with an in-stab and their union is a stabbing segment of $ P (D, \formula) $, Lemma~\ref{lem:variableGadgetStabbingN4}, Lemma~\ref{lem:splitGadgetStabbingN4}, and Lemma~\ref{lem:clauseGadgetStabbingN4} implies a consistent truth value assignment for all the variables, with the convention that the variable of an undetermined variable gadget is set to true (this convention is arbitrary).
Finally, by Lemma~\ref{lem:clauseGadgetStabbingN4} and because the values propagated in the gadgets does not increase, the clause must have at least one of its literals set to true. 


If the formula $ \formula $ is satisfiable, then there exists a variable assignment such that all the clauses of $ \formula$ evaluate to true. Here we use the constructions described in the proof of  Lemma~\ref{lem:variableGadgetStabbingN4}, Lemma~\ref{lem:splitGadgetStabbingN4}, and Lemma~\ref{lem:clauseGadgetStabbingN4} to obtain a minimal conforming partition of $ P (D, \formula) $ with stabbing number at most~$ 4 $. 








\subsection{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:NPHardStabbingN4+} When \texorpdfstring{$k=4$}{k=4} Using Polygons in General Position}\label{sec:4intractableGeneralPosition}


To prove the $ \NP $-hardness for the polygons in general position, we use the same technique as for proving the hardness results for thin polygons, but modify the gadgets. The functionalities of each gadget remains the same, therefore, we only give a high-level overview of the changes. 


\paragraph{Forcer gadget.}
We use a staircase with 6 reflex vertices as the force gadget, which is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:forcerGadgetGeneralN4}(a) and (c). We need the property that in any conforming partition of the gadget with stabbing number 4, the maximal stabbing segment $s$ perpendicular to the connection edge stabs 3 reflex segments.  Suppose for a contradiction that $s$ intersects smaller than three reflex segments. Then at least four vertical reflex segments in the partition would reach the topmost edge of the gadget implying a stabbing number higher than 4, a contradiction.  We also need the gadget to have a conforming partition with stabbing number at most 4, which is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:forcerGadgetGeneralN4}(b).


