\begin{abstract}
Power demand forecasting is a critical task for achieving efficiency and reliability in power grid operation. Accurate forecasting allows grid operators to better maintain the balance of supply and demand as well as to optimize operational cost for generation and transmission.
%the smart grid in terms of demand response and resource allocation.
This article proposes a novel neural network architecture \textit{PowerNet}, which can incorporate multiple heterogeneous features, such as historical energy consumption data, weather data, and calendar information, for the power demand forecasting task. 
Compared to two recent works based on Gradient Boosting Tree (GBT) and Support Vector Regression (SVR), PowerNet demonstrates a decrease of 
%28.58\% and 21.80\% 
33.3\% and 14.3\% 
in forecasting error, respectively.
%We further evaluate PowerNet by exploring different forecasting settings, including different forecasting frequencies and different forecasting periods. 
We further provide empirical results the two operational considerations that are crucial when using PowerNet in practice, 
%capability of PowerNet in terms of two questions, 
i.e., how far in the future the model can forecast with a decent accuracy and how often we should re-train the forecasting model to retain its modeling capability. 
Finally, we briefly discuss a multilayer anomaly detection approach based on PowerNet.
\end{abstract}


