\section{A comparison of progressive SR-ARAP performance in mesh deformation}
\label{appendix:progressive_sr_arap}

% By varying the parameter $\beta$ of the Inter-Step Smoothness term in our progressive SR-ARAP formulation (Eq.\ref{eq:sr_arap_interstep_energy}), we evaluate how strongly the mesh deformation preserves original geometry and maintains ARAP consistency across iterations. Our experiments (Table\ref{tab:supple_ablation_beta}) demonstrate that $\beta=0.8 $ effectively balances smooth transitions and geometric fidelity, yielding stable deformations with minimal distortions.

By varying the parameter $\beta$ of the Inter-Step Smoothness term in our progressive SR-ARAP formulation (Eq.\ref{eq:sr_arap_interstep_energy}), we evaluate how effectively the deformation preserves original geometry. To quantitatively assess these characteristics, we define two rigidity metrics: the Mean Edge Length Ratio (MELR) and the Mean ARAP Error ($\mathrm{mARAP_{Error}}$) (see Section\ref{appendix:rigidity_metrics} for detailed definitions).
%
The MELR measures the average ratio of edge lengths in the deformed mesh relative to their original lengths, indicating how well the deformation preserves the original geometry. The ARAP Error measures a simplified residual per triangle, reflecting how closely local rotations match between the original and deformed meshes.
%
We measure these metrics for $\beta$ values ranging from 0.2 to 1.0 to observe their effects on deformation quality. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:supple_ablation_beta}, $\beta=0.8$ effectively balances smooth transitions and rigidity preservation, yielding stable deformations with minimal distortions.


\subsection{Rigidity Metrics}
\label{appendix:rigidity_metrics}

To quantify how closely a deformed mesh $\hat{M} = (\hat{V}, F)$ preserves the original geometry $M = (V, F)$, we compute two metrics specifically for the vertices optimized through the energy function (i.e., movable vertices), as constraint vertices remain fixed during deformation.

\paragraph{Mean Edge Length Ratio (MELR).}
Let $\{(i, j)\}$ be the set of unique edges connecting movable vertices derived from the triangular faces in $M$. For each such edge $(i, j)$, we measure the ratio of its length in the deformed mesh to its length in the original mesh, as defined in Eq.~\ref{eq:melr_ratio}.

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:melr_ratio}
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{Ratio}_{ij}
&= \frac{\bigl\|\hat{v}_j - \hat{v}_i\bigr\|}
        {\bigl\|v_j - v_i\bigr\|}, \\
&\quad \text{where } v_i, v_j \in V_{\text{movable}},\; \hat{v}_i, \hat{v}_j \in \hat{V}_{\text{movable}},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

We then compute the mean edge length ratio (MELR), across all movable edges, as shown in Eq.~\ref{eq:melr_mean}. A MELR value closer to $1.0$ indicates minimal distortion of edge lengths.

\begin{equation}
\text{MELR} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{E}_{\text{movable}}|} \sum_{(i,j)\in \mathcal{E}_{\text{movable}}} \mathrm{Ratio}_{ij},
\label{eq:melr_mean}
\end{equation}

