\subsection{Real-world Hypergraphs}\label{sec:results:real_mixed_homophily}

To evaluate whether our findings on inequality in synthetic settings generalize to real-world contexts, we analyze a diverse set of hypergraphs drawn from domains including scientific collaboration, face-to-face interaction, and political decision-making. These datasets differ substantially in size, group composition, and structural features, reflecting the breadth of the diversity of complex real-world systems.

We analyze 9 datasets: Hospital~\cite{vanhems2013_Estimating}, High School~\cite{benson2018_SimplicialClosurePrediction, mastrandrea2015_ContactHighSchool}, Primary School~\cite{chodrow2021_GenerativeHypergraphClustering, stehl2011_ContactPrimarySchool}, Senate Bills (by party and by gender)~\cite{chodrow2021_GenerativeHypergraphClustering, fowler2006_ConnectingCongress, fowler2006_CosponsorshipHouseSenate}, House Bills (by party and by gender)~\cite{chodrow2021_GenerativeHypergraphClustering, fowler2006_ConnectingCongress, fowler2006_CosponsorshipHouseSenate}, DBLP~\cite{dblp}, and APS~\cite{aps_datasets}. Table~\ref{tab:real_world_overview} summarizes how nodes, hyperedges, and group labels are defined in each case, along with the number of nodes and the group-size composition. Additional details on the datasets, hypergraph construction, gender labeling with two widely-used gender labeling APIs (\texttt{GenderAPI}\footnote{\url{https://gender-api.com/}} and \texttt{genderize.io}),\footnote{\url{https://genderize.io/}} APS author disambiguation, and simulation parameters can be found in Appendices~\ref{appendix:real-world_hypergraph_construction}-\ref{appendix:additional_results_real_world}.

