%
In real-world hypergraphs, hyperedge homophily can vary greatly with hyperedge size. This dependence on hyperedge size can influence information access under different contagion dynamics. For the real-world hypergraphs in this analysis, we compute the hyperedge homophily $h_{s,r}^{(g)}$ (see Section~\ref{sec:methods:hypergraph_homophily}) for each majority/minority group, at varying hyperedge sizes and types (Fig.~\ref{fig:real_world_homophily}; additional datasets, hyperedge sizes, and alternative gender labels are presented in Appendix~\ref{appendix:datasets}). We find salient differences in hyperedge homophily across different hyperedge sizes, consistent with previous studies \cite{veldt2023_CombinatorialCharacterizationsImpossibilities, sarker2024_HigherorderHomophilySimplicial}, reflecting system-specific phenomena.

In the APS coauthorship hypergraph, all-male hyperedges are consistently overrepresented across hyperedge sizes, indicating persistent homophilous interactions among majority-group researchers (Fig.~\ref{fig:real_world_homophily}(a)). DBLP, another coauthorship hypergraph, shows overrepresentation of both all-male and all-female hyperedges, with stronger effects for the minority group (Fig.~\ref{fig:real_world_homophily}(b)). This suggests that, unlike in APS, female researchers may form more tightly connected clusters. These differences may surface due to our gender labeling approach or could reflect field-specific co-authorship norms.

Face-to-face interaction hypergraphs show different patterns possibly due to differences in social norms. In the High School hypergraph, homophilous gender interactions are prominent at smaller hyperedge sizes but give way to mixed-gender groups at size five (Fig.~\ref{fig:real_world_homophily}(c)). In contrast, the Primary School hypergraph (Appendix~\ref{appendix:datasets}) is consistently homophilous, even in larger interactions. These patterns suggest that the social dynamics that shape group formation in schools may depend on interaction size. The Hospital hypergraph exhibits a distinct pattern that combines heterophilous and homophilous interactions, reflecting healthcare dynamics (Fig.~\ref{fig:real_world_homophily}(d)). Most hyperedges contain a single patient, consistent with one-on-one care interactions. In contrast, larger hyperedges are often homophilous among healthcare workers, possibly reflecting collaboration or social interaction during breaks. 

We also observe mixed hyperedge homophily patterns in the U.S. House of Representatives dataset, both with party and gender node labels (Fig.~\ref{fig:real_world_homophily}(e),(f)). In the gender-labeled hypergraph, we observe a neutral pattern in male-only hyperedges, while female-only hyperedges are consistently overrepresented. This pattern may be influenced by the temporal structure of the data, which includes all individuals who have ever served in the U.S. House of Representatives---a body comprised only of men until 1916. As such, women’s co-sponsorship activity may naturally cluster in time. Similar hyperedge homophily patterns are observed in the Senate hypergraphs (Appendix~\ref{appendix:datasets}).

