We now turn from hypergraph structure to the outcomes of simulated contagion processes on real-world hypergraphs. Asymmetric transmission consistently produces the highest levels of inequality and the greatest variability across simulations (see Fig.~\ref{fig:emd_real-world}, comparing four contagion dynamics: symmetric linear, symmetric sublinear, symmetric superlinear, and asymmetric nonlinear; see Appendix~\ref{appendix:parameters_real_world} for additional details). In contrast, the three symmetric dynamics yield more stable outcomes. An exception is the High School hypergraph (Fig.~\ref{fig:emd_real-world}(b)), which exhibits low inequality under linear and superlinear contagion, but elevated inequality under sublinear dynamics. We attribute this increase to sublinear contagion stunting the spread in larger, more heterophilous hyperedges, thereby increasing the spread in homophilous pairwise edges.

We observe relatively low inequality in the APS (Fig.~\ref{fig:emd_real-world}(f)) and DBLP (Fig.~\ref{fig:emd_real-world}(i)) hypergraphs, in contrast to previous findings \cite{zappala2025_GenderDisparitiesDissemination}. We attribute this difference to methodological choices: whereas previous work excludes nodes with ambiguous gender, we retain these nodes and assign labels via probabilistic sampling. Our approach leads to more balanced group compositions than one might expect in the male-dominated fields of physics and computer science; however, maintaining these nodes allows for a more faithful representation of the underlying structure of interactions.

