\section{Synthetic Hypergraph Properties and Parameters}\label{appendix:parameters_synthetic}

Here we describe the parameters used in the experiments on synthetic hypergraphs, including the parameters of the hypergraph model, those of the social contagion model, and those of the seeding strategies. We also provide additional details on the simulations.

\paragraph{Hypergraph Model Parameters}
The inputs to the hypergraph model are the number of nodes $n_g$ in each group, the group-wise degree distributions $\rho_g(\kappa)$, and the number of hyperedges $m_{s,r}$ of size $s\in\{2,\dots,s_\mathrm{max}\}$ and type $r\in\{0,\dots,s\}$, where $s_\mathrm{max}$ is the largest hyperedge size. The type $r$ of a hyperedge is the number of group $g=1$ nodes contained in it.

In the main text, we provide results for \textit{group imbalanced} hypergraphs with $n_0=7500$ nodes in the majority group ($g=0$) and $n_1=2500$ nodes in the minority group ($g=1$). We focus on \textit{degree heterogeneous} hypergraphs with degrees sampled from a Pareto distribution $\mathrm{Pareto}[\bar{\kappa}_g,\gamma]$ with exponent $\gamma=2.9$ of the probability density function and means $\bar{\kappa}_g$ set equal to

\begin{equation}\label{eq:appendix:degree_consistency}
    \begin{split}
    \bar{\kappa}_0 &= \frac{1}{n_0}\sum_{s\in\{2,\dots,s_\mathrm{max}\}}\sum_{r\in\{0,\dots,s-1\}}(s-r)m_{s,r},\\
    \bar{\kappa}_1 &= \frac{1}{n_1}\sum_{s\in\{2,\dots,s_\mathrm{max}\}}\sum_{r\in\{1,\dots,s\}}r m_{s,r},\\
    \end{split}
\end{equation}
where the hyperedge counts $m_{s,r}$ are chosen according to a given hyperedge homophily pattern. 

In Appendix~\ref{appendix:additional_results_synthetic}, we present results for \textit{group balanced} hypergraphs with $n_0=n_1=5000$, as well as \textit{degree homogeneous} hypergraphs with $\kappa$ sampled from a Poisson distribution, $\rho_g(\kappa)=\mathrm{Poisson}[\bar{\kappa}_g]$ with mean $\bar{\kappa}_g$ determined by Equation~\eqref{eq:appendix:degree_consistency}.

We define three basic hyperedge homophily patterns using hyperedge homophily $h^{(g)}_{s,r}$. The corresponding hyperedge counts $m_{s,r}$ can be found in Table~\ref{tab:homophily_patterns}. We restrict our attention to non-uniform hypergraphs with hyperedges of sizes $s\in\{2,3,4\}$. In the \textit{neutral pattern}, hyperedge counts equal their expectation in a suitable null model, i.e., $h^{(g)}_{s,r}=1$ for all $s\in\{2,3,4\}$ and $r\in\{0,\dots,s\}$. In the \textit{homophilous pattern}, hyperedges that contain nodes of only one type are overrepresented $h^{(1)}_{s,s}=h^{(0)}_{s,0}=1.5$, and hyperedges that incorporate nodes of different types are underrepresented, i.e., $h^{(g)}_{s,r} = \tilde{h}_s^{(g)}<1$ for the relevant values of $s$ and $r$ and some hyperedge homophily value $\tilde{h}_s^{(g)}$. We choose $\tilde{h}_s^{(g)}$ to be independent of $r$. In the \textit{heterophilous pattern}, hyperedges that contain nodes of only one type are underrepresented $h^{(1)}_{s,s}=h^{(0)}_{s,0}=0.5$, while hyperedges that contain nodes of different types are overrepresented, i.e., $h^{(g)}_{s,r}=\tilde{h}^{(g)\prime}_{s}>1$, where $\tilde{h}^{(g)\prime}_{s}$ is independent of $r$. 

We also create \textit{mixed hyperedge homophily patterns} by selecting different combinations of the basic hyperedge homophily patterns for each hyperedge size $s$. We restrict ourselves to fixing one pattern for pairwise interactions ($s=2$), and another pattern for all higher-order interactions ($s>2$). For example, the homophily-neutral mixed pattern uses the homophilous basic pattern for the pairwise interactions and the neutral pattern for the higher-order edges. The resulting hyperedge counts are listed in Table~\ref{tab:homophily_patterns}.


