% Basic Summary Statistics
The real-world hypergraphs vary substantially in size, group composition, connectivity, and structural measures of inequality---including differences in node count, group proportions, hyperedge count, average degree, maximum hyperedge sizes, and degree-based differences (see Tab.~\ref{tab:real_world_data}). The hypergraphs also exhibit diverse hyperedge size distributions (see Fig.~\ref{fig:edgesize_distribution}). In addition to displaying summary information on the hypergraph structure, we include two structural inequality measures introduced by Avin et al.~\cite{avin2015_HomophilyGlassCeiling}: the power inequality, defined as the ratio of group-wise average degrees $\bar{k}_1/\bar{k}_0$, and the moment glass ceiling, defined as the ratio of group-wise second moments of the degree distributions $\langle k^2\rangle_1/\langle k^2\rangle_0$. In both cases, values below $1$ indicate a structural advantage for the majority group (assumed to be $g=0$).

The hypergraphs also display diverse, domain-specific hyperedge homophily patterns. The patterns for six of the real-world hypergraphs, APS (\texttt{GenderAPI}), DBLP (\texttt{GenderAPI}), House (Party), House (\texttt{GenderAPI}), High School, and Hospital, are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:real_world_homophily} and discussed in the main text (Section~\ref{sec:results:real_mixed_homophily}). The remaining hypergraphs, Primary School, Senate (Party), and Senate (Gender), have similar hyperedge homophily patterns (see Fig.~\ref{fig:homophily-SI}). The Primary School hypergraph (Fig.~\ref{fig:homophily-SI}(c)) is close to neutral for pairwise edges but becomes homophilous at larger hyperedge sizes, particularly for the minority group. The Senate hypergraph (Fig.~\ref{fig:homophily-SI}(e)) is strongly homophilous by party. In contrast, when labeled by gender with \texttt{GenderAPI}, the Senate hypergraph mirrors the pattern seen in the House: male-only groups follow the null model, while female-only groups are overrepresented (Fig.~\ref{fig:homophily-SI}(f)). The hyperedge homophily patterns observed in the \texttt{genderize.io}-labeled hypergraphs are qualitatively similar to those derived from \texttt{GenderAPI} (Fig.~\ref{fig:homophily-SI_genderizerio}).

