\section{Supplementary Results on Real-World Hypergraphs}\label{appendix:additional_results_real_world}

We present additional results for the real-world hypergraphs introduced in Section~\ref{sec:results:real_mixed_homophily}. The main text focuses on a single inequality measure---distributions of Wasserstein distance $d_W({\mathcal{Z}_0, \mathcal{Z}_1})$ between group-wise rank distributions---using \texttt{GenderAPI} labels and majority seeding. Here, we supplement those results with additional measures, seeding strategies, and gender-labeling to support three points: (1) time-based and fairness-based outcomes mirror our main-text findings, (2) results are robust to minority seeding, and (3) outcomes remain qualitatively consistent when node labels are generated using \texttt{genderize.io} instead of \texttt{GenderAPI}.

\FloatBarrier
\paragraph{Additional Results with \texttt{GenderAPI} and Majority Seeding}

In the main text, we quantify information access inequality on real-world hypergraphs using distributions of the Wasserstein distance $d_W(\mathcal{Z}_0, \mathcal{Z}_1)$ between group-wise rank distributions under majority seeding for hypergraphs with ground-truth and \texttt{GenderAPI} group labels. Here, we supplement those results with three additional measures: the time $t^{(g)}_{90}$ to reach $90$\% of a group $g$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_maj}), acquisition fairness $\alpha(f)$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_maj}), and diffusion fairness $\delta(f)$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world}). Together, these measures provide a more nuanced understanding of when and how inequality emerges in each setting.

The time $t^{(g)}_{90}$ to reach $90$\% of a group $g$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_maj}) reveals that symmetric dynamics typically yield low inequality for Primary School (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_maj}(a)), APS (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_maj}(f)), and DBLP (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_maj}(i)). Under asymmetric contagion, however, the majority gains a consistent timing advantage across all three. Larger and more consistent timing gaps appear in Hospital (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_maj}(c)), House (Party) (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_maj}(d)), House (Gender) (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_maj}(e)), Senate (Party) (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_maj}(g)), and Senate (Gender) (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_maj}(h)), where the majority group reaches $90$\% coverage earlier under all dynamics. The High School hypergraph exhibits a slight minority advantage under sublinear contagion, but a majority advantage under asymmetric dynamics (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_maj}(b)).

Acquisition fairness $\alpha(f)$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_maj}) highlights additional dynamics that are not fully captured by rank distributions or the time to inform $90$\% of nodes. APS (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_maj}(f)) and DBLP (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_maj}(i)) consistently exhibit a majority advantage, particularly under asymmetric contagion. House (Gender) (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_maj}(e)) and Senate (Gender) (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_maj}(h)) appear nearly neutral under symmetric dynamics (with a slight minority advantage in the former and slight majority advantage in the latter), but shift sharply toward a majority advantage under asymmetric contagion. Party-labeled hypergraphs (House and Senate in (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_maj}(d)) and (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_maj}(g)), respectively) show large swings early in the contagion process, but quickly settle into modest majority advantages, illustrating dynamics not evident in static or endpoint-based measures. High School (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_maj}(b)) and Primary School (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_maj}(a)) show early minority advantages under sublinear contagion, but ultimately favor the majority group under other dynamics. In Primary School, this early advantage fades quickly, while it persists for longer High School. Finally, the Hospital hypergraph is notable for beginning with a large minority advantage under superlinear and asymmetric dynamics, which rapidly reverses into a strong majority advantage (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_maj}(c)).

Diffusion fairness $\delta(f)$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world}) captures a group’s ability to initiate spread. APS (Fig.~\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world}(f)), DBLP (Fig.~\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world}(i)), and Hospital (Fig.~\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world}(c)) consistently show majority advantages, with the effect most pronounced under linear contagion in the latter case. Senate (Party) (Fig.~\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world}(g)) displays a minority advantage only under asymmetric contagion, while House (Party) (Fig.~\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world}(d)) shows the opposite trend. In Primary School (Fig.~\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world}(a)), the minority group gains an advantage under superlinear contagion, but the majority dominates under other dynamics. High School (Fig.~\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world}(b)) is nearly neutral overall, with only a small minority advantage at the beginning of the spreading process under sublinear dynamics and a small majority advantage under other contagion regimes. Interestingly, House (Gender) (Fig.~\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world}(e)) shows a consistent minority advantage across all dynamics, while Senate (Gender) (Fig.~\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world}(h)) shows a consistent majority advantage. In both cases, variability is extremely high. This is likely due to the severe group size imbalance and the clustered structure of the minority group, which cause outcomes to be highly sensitive to the choice of majority seed.

%time: genderapi + majority seeding
