\paragraph{Results with \texttt{GenderAPI} and Minority Seeding}

In the main text and the above section, we measure inequality under majority seeding for real-world hypergraphs labeled with \texttt{GenderAPI}. Here, we repeat the analysis using minority seeding to assess the robustness of our findings. We include results for the distributions of Wasserstein distance $d_W(\mathcal{Z}_0, \mathcal{Z}_1)$ for group-dependent rank distributions (Fig.~\ref{fig:emd_real-world_minority_seed}), the time $t^{(g)}_{90}$ to reach $90$\% of a group $g$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_min}), and acquisition fairness $\alpha(f)$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_min}). We do not include additional diffusion fairness plots, as $\delta(f)$ is computed using both seeding strategies by construction.

Overall, the patterns observed under minority seeding closely mirror those from the majority seeding condition, suggesting that our results are stable across reasonable seeding strategies. However, we identify a few notable exceptions.

First, under sublinear contagion, the High School hypergraph no longer exhibits a pronounced minority advantage in the order in which nodes are informed (Fig.~\ref{fig:emd_real-world_minority_seed}(b)). This shift is also reflected in the time $t^{(g)}_{90}$ it takes to inform $90$\% of the nodes (Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderapi_min}(f)).

Second, in the acquisition fairness plots, High School (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_min}(b)) now mirrors the behavior of Primary School (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_min}(a)): both display a transient minority advantage under sublinear contagion that rapidly converges toward neutrality. This pattern contrasts with the persistent minority advantage seen in High School under majority seeding (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_maj}(b)).

Third, in the Hospital hypergraph, acquisition fairness now exhibits a transient minority advantage under not only asymmetric and superlinear dynamics, as seen under majority seeding, but also under linear contagion (Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderapi_min}(c)). In all three cases, the advantage is short-lived and transitions quickly to a strong majority advantage, highlighting the sensitivity of early diffusion dynamics to the group initially seeded in settings with extreme group imbalance and mixed hyperedge homophily patterns.

Aside from these specific differences, all other trends remain qualitatively unchanged. These findings further support the robustness of our main conclusions and reinforce the importance of both hypergraph structure and contagion dynamics in shaping inequality in time-critical information access.

