\paragraph{Results with \texttt{genderize.io}}

In the main text, we assign gender labels to the House, Senate, APS, and DBLP hypergraphs using \texttt{GenderAPI}. Here, we assess the robustness of our findings to differences in gender labeling by replicating all simulations on these hypergraphs using labels generated with \texttt{genderize.io}. We use the approach outlined in Appendix~\ref{appendix:gender_labeling} to produce these labels.

Figures~\ref{fig:emd_real-world_genderizeio_maj}-\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world_genderizerio} summarize results for both majority and minority seeding. We explore distributions of Wasserstein distances $d_W(\mathcal{Z}_0, \mathcal{Z}_1)$ (majority seeding: Fig.~\ref{fig:emd_real-world_genderizeio_maj}; minority seeding: Fig.~\ref{fig:emd_real-world_genderizeio_min}), time to $90$\% informed nodes (majority seeding: Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderizerio_maj}; minority seeding: Fig.~\ref{fig:time_real-world_genderizerio_min}), acquisition fairness (majority seeding: Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderizerio_maj}; minority seeding: Fig.~\ref{fig:acquisition_real-world_genderizerio_min}), and diffusion fairness (Fig.~\ref{fig:diffusion_real-world_genderizerio}). The outcomes across all measures and dynamics are qualitatively consistent with those reported for the \texttt{GenderAPI} labels.

Together, these results reinforce the robustness of our gender labeling strategy and our conclusions: the key mechanisms driving information access inequality persist regardless of the gender labeling method used.

