\section{Discussions and Challenges}
\label{sec7}
\subsection{Comparison of Representations}
\label{sec6}
Building upon the above discussion, we categorize AI-based 3D reconstruction in radiological imaging into two broad classes: explicit and implicit representations. The explicit category includes traditional slice-based and volume-based methods, as well as emerging Gaussian-based representations. The implicit category encompasses implicit neural radiance fields and implicit neural embeddings. Each representation paradigm offers distinct advantages and disadvantages.

In this section, we evaluate these methods based on five key criteria: \textbf{effectiveness} (Effctv.), \textbf{efficiency} (Effcy.), \textbf{adaptability} (Adapt.), \textbf{simplicity} (Simpl.), and \textbf{interpretability} (Intrpt.). Each method is assigned a score from \textit{1} to \textit{5} for each criterion, where \textit{1} indicates poor performance and \textit{5} indicates relatively strong performance in the respective aspect. For comparison, we also include traditional probabilistic-based reconstruction algorithms (Iterative) in the evaluation. Detailed results are presented in Table \ref{tbl8}.
