As shown in Table 8, the slice-based method is limited in effectiveness due to its lack of spatial information. In contrast, Implicit Prior Embedding (IPE) integrates prior knowledge, enabling it to achieve better performance. Compared to implicit representations, explicit representations are generally more efficient. Notably, Gaussian representations, owing to their unique modeling approach, can achieve real-time reconstruction, offering promising potential for clinical real-time applications in radiological imaging.

Although novel representation methods such as Gaussian, IPE, and Neural Radiance Fields (NRF) demonstrate strong performance in terms of effectiveness, they exhibit limitations in adaptability compared to slice-based and volume-based methods. Researchers often need to develop task- or anatomy-specific algorithms, which also compromises simplicity. This may hinder clinical translation and deployment. Specifically, IPE relies on prior images, making it difficult to generalize across different clinical scenarios.

Moreover, all AI-based reconstruction approaches currently face significant challenges in terms of interpretability, especially when compared with traditional probabilistic iterative reconstruction methods.

\subsection{Imaging Quality Assessment}
With AI advancing 3D reconstruction in radiological imaging, methods have evolved from slice-based (\ref{sec:slice}) to volume-based (\ref{sec:volume}), Gaussian representation (\ref{sec:gr}), and implicit-based (\ref{sec5}) techniques, continuously improving metrics like PSNR and SSIM \citep{wang2004image}. However, medical imaging quality assessment must go beyond computational benchmarks to consider clinical relevance \citep{chow2016review}, including artifacts \citep{wuest2015improved}, registration accuracy \citep{ketcha2017effects}, and fidelity \citep{yang2023ct}. Clinician evaluation remains essential in complementing these metrics.

In the field of natural image quality assessment, numerous studies have demonstrated that combining Image Quality Assessment (IQA) \citep{athar2019comprehensive} with Image Quality Metrics (IQM) \citep{ahumada1993computational} —that is, integrating comprehensive survey evaluations with mathematical metrics—provides a more effective means of assessing image quality. This approach is particularly prevalent in image restoration tasks such as deblurring \citep{da2023image} and low-light enhancement \citep{kandula2023illumination,li2024lime}.

Similarly, some attempts have been made in the medical field \citep{kastryulin2023image,leveque2021comparative,mirza2023comparative}. We believe that extensive medical clinical evaluations hold greater practical value than achieving high metric scores.

At the same time, the adoption of more diverse and effective evaluation metrics can further enhance reconstruction quality \citep{yim2010quality}. Whether in natural images or medical imaging, selecting appropriate evaluation metrics can help guide models to learn deeper and more meaningful information \citep{almohammad2010stego,shaheed2024hybrid}.

\subsection{Efficiency \& Effectiveness}
Early convolutional neural networks enabled fast but basic reconstruction (\ref{sec:slice}), while advanced models such as diffusion models (\ref{sec:volume}) and NeRF (\ref{sec:nerf}) struggle with the trade-off between quality and speed. 3DGS (\ref{sec:gr}) addresses this issue by leveraging Gaussian representations and parallel computing for fast, high-fidelity reconstruction. However, achieving both rapid and high-quality results remains a key AI research focus in medical imaging \citep{zhou2021review,prevedello2019challenges}.

Transfer learning, which involves pretraining models on large-scale datasets and fine-tuning them for specific medical imaging tasks, effectively reduces training time and computational costs \citep{zhuang2020comprehensive}. In the medical field, transfer learning has been widely applied to classification \citep{kim2022transfer}, segmentation \citep{karimi2021transfer}, and other tasks \citep{raghu2019transfusion}.  Notably, the emergence of Segment Anything Model (SAM) \citep{kirillov2023segment} has demonstrated precise medical image segmentation through fine-tuning \citep{ma2024segment}. Unlike classification and segmentation, radiological image reconstruction relies on the unique attributes of medical images, making direct application challenging \citep{zhang2017deep,knoll2019assessment}. Instead, embedding imaging-specific knowledge is essential for achieving optimal results.

Another promising approach is edge computing, which offloads computationally intensive tasks to edge devices or cloud platforms, enabling real-time and distributed AI processing \citep{chen2019deep}. In large language models, edge computing has been widely adopted \citep{zheng2025review}. In the field of radiological image reconstruction, Isosalo et al. \citep{isosalo2023local} have explored the feasibility of local edge computing for radiological image reconstruction and computer-aided detection. Zhang et al. \citep{zhang20213d} utilized edge computing in IoT-based 3D telemedicine for denoising CT images. Additionally, Zhang et al. \citep{zhang2025collaborative} proposed a collaborative inference strategy for medical image diagnosis in a mobile edge computing environment. We believe that integrating radiological image reconstruction with edge computing holds great clinical value, offering potential solutions for real-time imaging.

Meanwhile, advancements in AI-driven low-dose and sparse-view reconstruction have reduced medical imaging costs, though the primary expense now lies in the computational resources required for high-performance AI models. To further reduce costs, developing lightweight end-to-end models with fewer parameters is crucial. Additionally, knowledge distillation has emerged as a popular solution \citep{gou2021knowledge}. In medical segmentation, Zhou et al. \citep{zhou2024distillation} proposed a one-shot medical image segmentation framework based on image reconstruction-guided distillation learning, effectively reducing computational demands. In the context of medical imaging reconstruction, Wu et al. \citep{wu2024adaptive} introduced an unsupervised MRI reconstruction method based on self-supervised training, knowledge distillation, and sample reweighting. By training a teacher model and distilling its knowledge into a student model, their approach accelerated inference while improving performance across multiple datasets.

\subsection{Privacy Protection}
Given the sensitivity of medical imaging data, secure AI-driven reconstruction is crucial \citep{williamson2024balancing}. To address this challenge, numerous solutions have been proposed.

First, various unsupervised models have been developed, incorporating Gaussian mixture models \citep{li2022noise}, unbiased risk estimation \citep{aggarwal2022ensure}, or 3DGS-based \citep{cai2024structureawaresparseviewxray3d,cai2025radiative} architectures. These algorithms operate without requiring training datasets or external patient information \citep{dike2018unsupervised}, providing a promising approach to enhancing patient privacy.

Federated Learning ensures privacy preservation by training models on decentralized data sources without transferring sensitive patient data \citep{guan2024federated}. This approach has been successfully applied to MRI reconstruction, achieving results that closely match multi-institutional data distributions \citep{feng2022specificity,elmas2022federated}.

Additionally, domain adaptation \citep{guan2021domain,han2018deep} and domain generalization \citep{yoon2024domain} techniques help mitigate domain shifts between public datasets and clinical data, preventing the need for direct training on clinical datasets. Recently, Test-Time Training has emerged as a focal point in this area \citep{zhao2024test,he2021autoencoder}.

Moreover, integrating differential privacy into AI models ensures that individual data points remain protected during the reconstruction process \citep{ziller2021medical}. The application of homomorphic encryption can further enable computations on encrypted medical data without compromising privacy \citep{yang2019secure,dutil2021application}.

Blockchain-based secure data sharing has also gained attention \citep{xi2022review}, as it ensures the secure and transparent exchange of medical imaging data while safeguarding patient confidentiality \citep{shen2019privacy}.


\subsection{Model Interpretability}
The opacity of complex AI-based reconstruction models limits clinical trust \citep{eke2024role}. To improve interpretability, recent models decompose reconstruction into traceable steps \citep{salahuddin2022transparency}. Current methods mainly fall into two categories: ante-hoc interpretability \citep{li2018tell,li2018deep} and post-hoc interpretability \citep{selvaraju2017grad,sundararajan2017axiomatic}.

In the field of radiological image reconstruction, Zhang et al. proposed DUN-SA by unfolding the iterative stages of an optimization algorithm into network modules with clear physical meanings \citep{zhang2025deep}. This approach makes the learning process more interpretable and addresses the lack of explainability in deep learning-based multimodal MRI reconstruction.

We believe that improving the interpretability of radiological image reconstruction models will accelerate the clinical deployment of AI.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec8}
Since the development of computer vision technology in artificial intelligence, its applications in medical imaging analysis have become increasingly widespread. This review primarily examines its applications in 3D reconstruction for radiological imaging, covering multiple imaging modalities, including CT, MRI, PET, SPECT, and US. Over the past five years, there has been a substantial increase in research articles on artificial intelligence-based 3D reconstruction algorithms for radiological imaging.

This review categorizes existing literature into explicit and implicit reconstruction methods based on imaging approaches. It further provides a comprehensive discussion of the development, advantages, and limitations of these two representation paradigms in the field.

Given the unique characteristics of medical imaging, artificial intelligence algorithms must balance multiple factors, including image quality, computational efficiency, and imaging cost. As current algorithmic trends evolve, an increasing number of researchers are expected to propose more efficient reconstruction methods that enhance medical image quality within limited computational time and cost. In the future, artificial intelligence is anticipated to enable low-cost, high-quality medical image reconstruction through end-to-end systems, facilitating rapid and cost-effective imaging solutions.


